from public.courses c where c.id = l.course_id and c.slug = 'chatgpt-operator' and l.slug = 'set-up-a-project-that-remembers-the-work';

update public.lessons l set content = $lesson$# CG L02: Give ChatGPT a Goal, Context, Output, and Boundaries

## What you will finish

Write prompts with a goal, context, output format, and boundaries so useful results arrive with less back-and-forth. When you are done you will have one vague prompt from your own history rewritten into four labeled parts, with the old result next to the new result so you can see exactly what changed.

## Why this matters

"Write me an email about fall aeration." That is a real prompt a lawn care owner in Gilmer showed me. He typed it at nine at night after a full day on the mower. What came back opened with "Transform your lawn this autumn!" and offered a discount he never mentioned. He closed the laptop and went to bed.

The tool did not fail. The prompt did. ChatGPT filled every gap he left with the most average thing on the internet. When you do not say who the reader is, it guesses. When you do not say what not to claim, it claims.

The fix is not a magic phrase. It is four parts, in order, every time. Goal, context, output, boundaries. Ten minutes writing the prompt saves forty minutes of "no, not like that." After a few weeks it takes two minutes because the frame is in your head.

This lesson comes second because the Project from lesson one now holds your durable facts. The prompt only has to carry what is specific to this one task. That is why it stays short.

## The lesson

### 1. State the result

The goal is what must be finished. Not what you want help with. Finished means you could hand it to a person and they could use it.

"Help me with an email" is not a goal. "Goal: a 120-word email to past customers, ready to paste into my email tool, that gets them to book fall aeration by replying or calling" is a goal. It names the piece, the reader, and the action the reader takes.

Put the reader in the goal. Put the action in the goal. If you cannot say what the reader should do after reading, you are not ready to write yet, and that is worth knowing before you spend the time.

What good looks like: one sentence, and a stranger could tell you when it is done.

The mistake is writing activities instead of results. "Brainstorm ideas." "Give me some options." Those keep you in the chat for an hour. If you really need options, make that the result: "Goal: three subject lines, each under 45 characters, for the email below."

Example: a dentist in Kilgore wants to remind lapsed patients to book a cleaning. Goal: "A friendly 80-word text message, sent from the office number, that gets a patient who has not visited in 18 months to call and book a cleaning."

### 2. Supply only relevant context

Context is the facts, the audience, and the source material for this specific task. Your project instructions already carry who you are and how you talk. Do not paste that again. Add what is new: the offer, the date, the price if you are publishing one, the season, the reader's situation, and any real material you have.

Paste real material instead of describing it. If a customer asked a question, paste the question. If you have notes from the job, paste the notes. ChatGPT works better from your actual words than from your summary of your words.

The rule for what to include: if the fact would change the output, include it. If it would not, leave it out. Then label where each fact came from, the same way you did in your project instructions. "Price: $85 per yard up to a quarter acre (source: my 2026 price sheet)."

What good looks like: five to ten lines of facts, each one true today, none of them decoration.

The mistake runs both directions. Some people paste their whole website and a three-year-old brochure, so the draft carries old prices. Others paste nothing and then get mad when it guesses. Relevant means the middle.

Example: for the aeration email, context is the service, the price, the booking method, the towns served, when the season runs, and the fact that the reader used the company before. That is it.

### 3. Specify format and limits

Output is the exact shape of the thing. Format, length, sections. "Output: one subject line plus a 120-word body. Plain text. One link placeholder written as [BOOKING LINK]. No bullet points. No sign-off longer than my name." Say it the way you would say it to a new hire who has never seen your emails.

Boundaries are what it must not do and what it must ask. Four kinds, and the prompt below has all four. Facts not to invent: no prices, reviews, statistics, or awards that are not in the context. Claims to avoid: no "best in East Texas," no results promises, nothing about the weather. Tools or sources required: "use only the fact sheet in this project." Questions to ask: the last line of the prompt makes ChatGPT list any missing fact that would materially change the result before it drafts.

That last line is the cheapest quality control you will ever run. Read the list, answer what matters, say "go." Skip it and the gaps get filled with guesses.

What good looks like: you can hand the output to someone without editing the shape, and nothing in it makes a claim you would not say to a customer's face.

The mistake is skipping boundaries because they feel negative. Then the draft says "over 500 five-star reviews" and you never had that many. Boundaries are not pessimism. They are the part of the prompt that protects your name.

Example: "Boundaries: do not invent a discount. Do not mention frost dates. Use only the facts above. Before drafting, list any missing fact."

Put the four parts together and you have the whole prompt. Keep the labels. They make the prompt readable by you in six months and by whoever you hand the business to.

## Worked example

Gilmer Lawn and Land is a fictional example. Marcus runs it with one crew. His vague prompt was "write an email about fall aeration."

Here is the rewrite he used inside his project chat. Goal: a 120-word email to past customers that gets them to book fall aeration by replying or calling. Context: core aeration, $85 per yard up to a quarter acre (source: 2026 price sheet), Gilmer and Gladewater, booking by phone or reply, season runs September through October, reader used us in the last two years. Output: one subject line under 45 characters plus a 120-word plain text body, one call to action, no bullets. Boundaries: do not invent discounts, reviews, or results; do not mention weather or frost dates; use only these facts; before drafting, list any missing fact that would materially change the result.

ChatGPT came back with three missing facts: the phone number, whether overseeding is included, and whether there is a booking deadline. He answered: office number, overseeding is a separate quote, no deadline. Then it drafted.

Example output:

```text
Subject: Fall aeration for your yard
Hi [First name],
You had us out in the last couple of years, so you know the drill. Fall is when core aeration does the most good for a Gilmer lawn.
It is $85 per yard up to a quarter acre. Overseeding is a separate quote if you want it.
Reply to this email or call 903-555-0127 and we will get you on the schedule for September or October.
Marcus
```

He checked it against the pass standard. Could a new reader find the goal, the trusted context, the expected output, and the boundaries without guessing? Yes, because the labels are right there in the prompt.

The one revision: the first draft opened with "before the first freeze hits in late October." He had said no frost dates, but the draft found another way to predict weather. He added "no seasonal predictions of any kind" to the boundaries and reran it. Clean.

He put the old result and the new result side by side. The old one was longer, invented a 15 percent discount, and never gave a phone number.

## Exact working prompt

```text
Goal: [what must be finished]. Context: [facts, audience, source material]. Output: [exact format, length, and sections]. Boundaries: [facts not to invent, claims to avoid, tools or sources required, and questions to ask]. Before drafting, list any missing fact that would materially change the result.
```

## Guided practice

1. Find one prompt from your own ChatGPT history that gave you something you could not use.
2. Rewrite it with the four labels using the four-part prompt card from the workbook.
3. Run it in a chat inside your project, answer the missing facts it lists, then say go.
4. Paste the old result and the new result into one document, side by side.
5. Write one line under them: what changed, and which part of the prompt caused it.

## Assignment and evidence

Rewrite one vague prompt with all four parts and compare the first result to the revised result.

Save the source material, the prompt, the first result, the final result, and your review notes.

## Pass standard

A new reader can identify the goal, trusted context, expected output, and non-negotiable boundaries without guessing.

## Common mistakes

- Writing an activity as the goal. Fix: name the finished piece, the reader, and the action the reader takes.
- Pasting the whole website as context. Fix: include only facts that would change this output, and label each source.
- Leaving out the format. Fix: state length, sections, and what a placeholder looks like.
- Skipping boundaries because they feel negative. Fix: list what not to invent and what not to claim, every time.
- Ignoring the missing-facts list. Fix: answer it before you let it draft.

## Recording plan

**Target runtime:** 5 to 8 minutes.
**Primary visual:** Goal-context-output-boundaries prompt frame.
**Screen recording:** Type the vague prompt and show its result, then type the four-part version live in a project chat, show the missing-facts reply, answer it, and put both results side by side on screen.
**Course map:** the course visual below anchors where this lesson sits.

![The ChatGPT Operator course visual](/images/academy/chatgpt-operator.svg)

## Downloadable

[Open the ChatGPT Operator workbook](/downloads/operator-academy/chatgpt-operator-workbook.pdf)

Workbook section: OA01 ChatGPT Operator Workbook, Four-part prompt card.

## Lesson check

Complete the practice, mark the assignment, and score at least 80 percent on the objective lesson check. Retakes are allowed. Major builds and capstones require reviewer approval before the completion credential can be issued.
$lesson$
from public.courses c where c.id = l.course_id and c.slug = 'chatgpt-operator' and l.slug = 'give-chatgpt-goal-context-output-and-boundaries';

update public.lessons l set content = $lesson$# CG L03: Build Your First Business Content Kit

## What you will finish

Turn one approved business fact set into a coordinated social post, email, short video script, and call to action. When you are done you will have one saved source brief and five finished pieces that all say the same true thing, each shaped for its channel, in one document you can share.

## Why this matters

Here is how most small business content gets made. Monday, a Facebook post written on the phone in the truck. Wednesday, an email from a template that still has last year's price. Friday, a video shot in the parking lot that mentions a deal nobody else has heard of. Three pieces, three different stories, one confused customer.

A gym owner in Tyler showed me her month. The post said the fall program started the 15th. The email said the 22nd. She had changed the date and updated one, not the other. Two people showed up on the wrong day.

The fix is to write the facts once, approve them once, and make every piece from that one page. That page is the source brief. It is the most boring document in this course and the one that saves you the most embarrassment.

This is the first deliverable lesson, and it closes the Beginner level. Lessons one and two gave you the project and the prompt frame. Now you use both to ship something a customer will actually see.

## The lesson

### 1. Approve one source brief

The source brief is one page. Type it yourself, in your project chat or in a document. It has six parts.

The one idea. What is this content about? One thing. A fall program, a spring drain special, a new location, a hiring push. Not three things.

The facts. What, who it is for, when, where, and how to respond. If you are publishing a price, the price. If there is a limit, the real limit. Every fact tagged with where it came from, the way you learned in lesson two.

The next action. One. Call this number. Text this word to this number. Click this link. Not "call, text, message, or stop by." Pick one and the whole kit points at it.

The voice. Three words and one sample sentence you actually said. "Plain, warm, no shouting. Example: 'We start at six because that is when most of you can make it.'"

The off-limits list. Claims you will not make. No results promises, no fake urgency, no testimonials you do not have written permission to use.

Approved means you read it slowly and every fact is true today. Then save it with a name you can find: brief-fall-strength-program-2026-09. Add it to the project files or paste it into the chat.

What good looks like: someone else could make the content from this page without asking you anything.

The mistake is skipping the brief and "just telling ChatGPT about it" in the prompt. Then the facts live in a chat you will never find again, and next month's kit starts from memory.

### 2. Adapt the idea to each channel

The exact working prompt below turns the brief into five pieces in one pass. One Facebook post, one Instagram caption, one 90-word email, one 30-second direct-to-camera script, and one call to action that is identical across all five.

Here is what each channel wants, so you can judge what comes back.

The Facebook post reads like you talking to people who already know you. Sixty to 120 words. Opens with the situation, not the offer. Ends on the next action.

The Instagram caption is shorter, broken into short lines so it reads on a phone, with three to five local hashtags at most. Same facts.

The email has a subject line under about 45 characters and a 90-word body. One link or one phone number. Your name at the bottom, not "The Team."

The 30-second script is 75 to 85 words spoken. First line is the hook, said the way you would say it to a camera in your shop. Middle is the offer. Last line is the action. No stage directions you will not follow.

The call to action is one sentence, word for word the same in all five. "Text START to 903-555-0142 to hold a spot."

Run the prompt in a new chat inside your project. Name the chat for the kit. Paste the brief where the prompt says [paste], and describe the voice where it says [describe] using your three words and the sample sentence.

The mistake is letting each piece grow its own facts. The email gets a bonus, the post gets a different date, the script adds "limited time." One brief, one prompt, one pass keeps them aligned.

Example: a church in Marshall runs the prompt for a fall food drive. Same date, same drop-off address, same phone number in all five pieces, because they all came from one page.

### 3. Run a consistency review

Do not post anything yet. Put all five pieces in one document and read them in a row. You are checking two things.

Same truth. Same date, same price, same phone number, same limit, same next action in all five. Circle anything that appears in one piece and not in the brief. That is an invented claim, and it goes.

Channel fit. Does the Facebook post read as a wall of text? Does the caption break into lines? Is the subject line too long? Would you actually say the script out loud, or does it sound like a commercial?

Then make one revision request, and make it targeted. "Revise only the script. It is 110 words. Cut to 80, keep the phone number, end on the text instruction." Do not say "make it better." Say what is wrong and what fixed looks like.

Read the revised piece once more against the brief. Save the final brief and the five outputs together in one document. That document is your evidence for this lesson.

What good looks like: a customer who sees the post on Tuesday and the email on Thursday hears one story.

The mistake is reviewing one piece at a time. The mismatches only show up when they sit next to each other.

Example: a detailer in Kilgore reviews her kit and finds the email says "this week only" and the brief says nothing about a deadline. She cuts it. Her customers do not get a fake countdown, and she does not have to explain it next week.

## Worked example

Iron Oak Fitness in Tyler is a fictional example. Dana owns it. Her brief: a six-week small-group strength program, starts September 15, mornings at 6, eight spots, $180 for the six weeks (source: her fall price sheet), for adults who have never lifted with a coach. Next action: text START to 903-555-0142 to hold a spot. Voice: plain, encouraging, no shouting. Off-limits: no results promises, no before-and-after claims, no urgency beyond the real eight spots.

She opens a chat in her project named "Fall strength kit," pastes the exact working prompt, drops the brief into [paste], and writes "plain, encouraging, no exclamation points, sounds like a coach talking after class" in [describe].

Example output:

```text
Facebook post: New to lifting and want a coach in the room? Our six-week small-group strength program starts September 15, mornings at 6. Eight people, one coach, $180 for the six weeks. Text START to 903-555-0142 to hold a spot.
Instagram caption: Six weeks. Eight spots. One coach. Starts Sept 15 at 6 a.m. Spots going fast. Text START to 903-555-0142 to hold a spot. #TylerTX #IronOakFitness
Email subject: Six-week strength program starts Sept 15
Script opening: If you have been meaning to start lifting but did not want to figure it out alone, this one is for you.
Call to action: Text START to 903-555-0142 to hold a spot.
```

Review against the pass standard. All five match the same facts and next action? The date, the price, the number, and the eight spots matched in every piece. But the caption had added "spots going fast," which is urgency that was not in the brief. And the email body said "you will feel stronger in two weeks," which is a results promise. Two circles.

The one revision: "Revise only the Instagram caption and the email. Remove 'spots going fast' and the two-week line. Keep everything else." It came back clean. She saved the brief and the five pieces in one Google Doc named "Iron Oak fall strength kit, final," set it to anyone with the link can view, and that is what she submits.

## Exact working prompt

```text
Using only this approved business brief: [paste], create a content kit with one Facebook post, one Instagram caption, one 90-word email, one 30-second direct-to-camera script, and one consistent call to action. Keep the voice [describe]. Do not add claims, statistics, testimonials, or urgency that are not in the brief.
```

## Guided practice

1. Write your one-page source brief using the content kit builder section of the workbook, and tag every fact with its source.
2. Open a new chat in your project, name it for the kit, and run the exact working prompt with your brief and voice filled in.
3. Paste all five pieces into one document and circle any fact or claim that is not in the brief.
4. Make one targeted revision request that names the piece, the problem, and what fixed looks like.
5. Save the final brief and the five outputs together and set the document to viewable by link.

## Assignment and evidence

Produce the five-piece kit, revise it once, and save the final source brief with the outputs.

Save the source material, the prompt, the first result, the final result, and your review notes. This is a designated deliverable lesson. Use the submission panel below to send the viewable link for review.

## Pass standard

All five pieces match the same facts and next action while fitting their individual channels.

## Common mistakes

- Skipping the brief and describing the offer in the prompt. Fix: write the one page first, approve it, then prompt from it.
- Three different next actions across the kit. Fix: pick one and paste it word for word into every piece.
- Accepting urgency ChatGPT added. Fix: if the deadline or limit is not in the brief, cut it.
- Reviewing pieces one at a time. Fix: put all five in one document and read them in a row.
- Asking for "make it better." Fix: name the piece, the defect, and the target.

## Recording plan

**Target runtime:** 5 to 8 minutes.
**Primary visual:** One source to five content assets.
**Screen recording:** Show the one-page brief, paste it into the prompt in a project chat, scroll the five pieces, paste them into one document, circle the two invented claims, and run the targeted revision.
**Course map:** the course visual below anchors where this lesson sits.

![The ChatGPT Operator course visual](/images/academy/chatgpt-operator.svg)

## Downloadable

[Open the ChatGPT Operator workbook](/downloads/operator-academy/chatgpt-operator-workbook.pdf)

Workbook section: OA01 ChatGPT Operator Workbook, Content kit builder.

## Lesson check

Complete the practice, mark the assignment, and score at least 80 percent on the objective lesson check. Retakes are allowed. Major builds and capstones require reviewer approval before the completion credential can be issued.
