update public.lessons l set content = $lesson$# CG L04: Create and Refine a Professional Image

## What you will finish

Create a professional profile or campaign image with a clear purpose, composition, style, text rule, and revision process. When you are done you will have one finished image saved at the right shape for where it is going, plus the prompt and the one revision note that got it there.

## Why this matters

Look at the Google Business Profile for most local businesses. The profile photo is a logo squeezed into a circle, or a phone selfie from 2018, or nothing. The Facebook cover is a stock photo of a handshake. People decide whether to call in about three seconds, and this is what they see.

A realtor in Henderson told me she had been meaning to get headshots for two years. A photographer costs money, takes a morning, and she never scheduled it. Meanwhile her profile picture was cropped out of a group shot at a closing.

ChatGPT can make images now, and it can work from a photo you upload. That does not replace a photographer for everything. It does get you a clean, honest profile image and a usable campaign graphic this afternoon.

The catch is that most people prompt it like a wish. "Make me a professional headshot." What comes back has a strange hand, a garbled sign in the background, and a jawline that is not yours. This lesson is the process that gets you to something you would actually post. It opens the Intermediate level, where the project and prompt skills start turning into assets.

## The lesson

### 1. Define intended use

Before you type anything, answer one question: where is this image going?

A profile picture is a small circle. Google, Facebook, LinkedIn, your email signature. Face centered, head and shoulders, plain background, because at forty pixels wide nothing else survives.

A campaign image is a rectangle. A Facebook ad, a post, a website banner. It needs room for a headline that you will add later, so the subject sits to one side and the other side stays open.

A cover image is wide and short, and on some platforms the middle gets covered by your profile circle.

The use decides the crop, the shape, and how much empty space you need. Say it in the prompt: "square profile picture for Google Business Profile" or "landscape marketing image for a Facebook post about spring cleanups, with the left third open for a headline."

What good looks like: you can name the platform, the shape, and what gets placed on top of the image before you generate it.

The mistake is making a beautiful image and then discovering it is the wrong shape. You crop it and lose the subject. Decide the use first.

Example: a pressure washer in Hallsville needs a post image. He asks for landscape, subject on the right, a clean wet driveway in the foreground, left side open.

### 2. Describe subject and composition

Now build the prompt. The exact working prompt below has the slots. Fill them like you are describing the shot to a photographer who has never met you.

Subject. Who or what, doing what, where. "A woman in her forties in a navy blazer, standing in front of a brick storefront, relaxed, looking at the camera." For a real person, upload a reference photo first. Click the plus button in the message box, add the photo, and say in the prompt that a reference is supplied. The constraint about preserving natural facial proportions matters here. You want it to look like you, not like a cousin.

Composition. Crop, placement, negative space. "Head and shoulders, centered, plain background" for a profile. "Subject right third, left two-thirds open, soft background" for a campaign image.

Style. Photorealistic, editorial, or illustration. Pick one. For a profile picture, photorealistic. For a campaign image, an editorial photo or a clean illustration both work.

Lighting and mood. "Soft natural light, late afternoon, calm." Colors. "Warm off-white background, navy and brass accents." Keep the palette to two or three colors so it matches your site.

Constraints. Keep the ones in the prompt. No invented logos, because it will make one up and it will be wrong. No watermark. No text unless quoted verbatim, because AI images misspell things, and even quoted text needs checking letter by letter. My rule: if the image needs words, add them later in Canva or Figma.

To generate, paste the prompt into the chat. If your tools menu shows Create image, you can pick it, but describing the image usually starts it on its own. Ask for one polished direction, not four. Four thumbnails means you pick without looking closely at any of them.

The mistake is describing the feeling instead of the picture. "Professional and trustworthy" is not a subject. "Standing beside her work truck with the door lettering out of frame" is.

Example: a gym in Tyler asks for an editorial photo of an empty squat rack in morning light, cream wall, one cobalt band on the floor, nothing else. It gets a clean campaign image with room for a headline.

### 3. Revise one variable at a time

The image comes back. Before you react to whether you like it, inspect it.

Face. Does it look like you, or like a smoothed-over version? Eyes even, teeth normal, ears matching. Hands. Count fingers. Check where they touch things. Text. Any sign, badge, or lettering in the background will be garbled. Edges. Hair against the background, glasses frames, where the shoulder meets the blazer. Brand fit. Do the colors and setting match your website and your other photos, or does it look like a different company?

Write down what is wrong. Then change one thing. "Keep everything the same. Remove the sign in the background and make the wall plain warm white." Wait. Inspect again. Then the next thing.

If you ask for three changes at once, it re-imagines the whole image and you lose what was working. One variable, one pass.

Download the final with the download control on the image. Name the file for its use: renee-profile-google-2026-09.png. If you need an exact pixel size, resize it in whatever tool you already use.

One honest sentence and we move on. Do not present a generated picture as a real photo of your team, your shop, or your finished work. A generated headshot built from your own photo is fine. A generated "before and after" of a driveway you never cleaned is not.

What good looks like: you could put the image next to your real photos and nothing about it would make a customer pause.

The mistake is settling for the first image because it is close. Close is what people notice. Fix the hand.

Example: a church in Marshall makes an illustration for a fall event post. First image has a garbled banner. Second pass removes the banner. Third pass warms the palette. Done in three prompts.

## Worked example

Cherokee Trace Realty in Henderson is a fictional example. Renee is the broker. She needs one profile picture that works on Google Business Profile, Facebook, and her email signature. She has a decent phone photo of herself in front of her office, but the lighting is harsh and there is a trash can in the shot.

She opens a chat in her project named "Profile picture," uploads the phone photo with the plus button, and fills in the exact working prompt. Purpose: square profile picture for Google Business Profile and Facebook. Subject: the woman in the reference photo, same face, same hair, navy blazer, relaxed, looking at the camera. Composition: head and shoulders, centered, plain background, face fills about half the frame. Style: photorealistic. Lighting and mood: soft natural window light, calm, approachable. Colors: warm off-white background, navy blazer, no other strong colors. Constraints as written. One polished direction.

Example output:

```text
Here is one polished direction based on your reference photo.
The image shows a head-and-shoulders portrait, centered, warm off-white background, soft window light from the left, navy blazer.
I preserved the facial structure and hair from your reference and removed the background elements.
No text, logos, or watermark were added.
Let me know if you would like a different crop, background tone, or lighting.
```

She inspects it. Face: close, but the smile shows more teeth than her real smile does. Hands: not in frame, good. Text: none. Edges: hair against the background is clean. Brand fit: the navy matches her site.

Review against the pass standard. Fits the intended placement: square, centered, reads at small size. Avoids visible artifacts: one, the smile. Follows every identity and text constraint: yes.

The one targeted revision: "Keep everything exactly the same. Match the mouth and smile to the reference photo more closely, lips slightly closed." The second image comes back with her real smile. She downloads it, names it renee-profile-2026-09.png, and uploads it to Google and Facebook the same afternoon.

## Exact working prompt

```text
Create a professional [profile picture or marketing image] for [purpose]. Subject: [describe]. Composition: [crop, placement, negative space]. Style: [photorealistic, editorial, illustration]. Lighting and mood: [describe]. Colors: [palette]. Constraints: preserve natural facial proportions if a reference is supplied, no invented logos, no watermark, and no text unless quoted verbatim. Produce one polished direction.
```

## Guided practice

1. Write down where the image is going and what shape that place needs.
2. If it is a picture of you, pick one clear reference photo and upload it in a new project chat.
3. Fill every slot in the exact working prompt and generate one direction.
4. Inspect face, hands, text, edges, and brand fit, and write down what is wrong.
5. Make one targeted revision, inspect again, download, and name the file for its use.

## Assignment and evidence

Generate one image, inspect the face, hands, text, edges, and brand fit, then make one targeted revision.

Save the source material, the prompt, the first result, the final result, and your review notes.

## Pass standard

The image fits its intended placement, avoids visible artifacts, and follows every identity and text constraint.

## Common mistakes

- Generating before deciding where the image goes. Fix: name the platform and the shape first.
- Describing a feeling instead of a picture. Fix: subject, setting, crop, placement, light, colors.
- Asking for text inside the image. Fix: add words later in Canva or Figma, or quote them exactly and check every letter.
- Requesting three fixes at once. Fix: one variable per pass, inspect between passes.
- Posting a generated image as a real photo of your work. Fix: use generated images for portraits and graphics, not for proof.

## Recording plan

**Target runtime:** 5 to 8 minutes.
**Primary visual:** Purpose-subject-composition-style-constraints image frame.
**Screen recording:** Upload a reference photo in a project chat, fill the prompt slot by slot, generate one image, zoom in on face, hands, and edges, run the one-variable revision, and download the final.
**Course map:** the course visual below anchors where this lesson sits.

![The ChatGPT Operator course visual](/images/academy/chatgpt-operator.svg)

## Downloadable

[Open the ChatGPT Operator workbook](/downloads/operator-academy/chatgpt-operator-workbook.pdf)

Workbook section: OA01 ChatGPT Operator Workbook, Image prompt and QA.

## Lesson check

Complete the practice, mark the assignment, and score at least 80 percent on the objective lesson check. Retakes are allowed. Major builds and capstones require reviewer approval before the completion credential can be issued.
$lesson$
from public.courses c where c.id = l.course_id and c.slug = 'chatgpt-operator' and l.slug = 'create-and-refine-a-professional-image';

update public.lessons l set content = $lesson$# CG L05: Write Social Posts and Emails That Sound Human

## What you will finish

Write channel-specific posts and emails that preserve the operator's real voice, story, and next action. When you are done you will have one Facebook post and one email built from the same real source note, both read aloud, with three specific changes marked that made them sound like you.

## Why this matters

You can spot an AI post from across the room. "Exciting news!" "We are thrilled to announce." "Elevate your experience." Your customers can spot it too, and it costs you something you cannot buy back: the sense that a real person is on the other end.

A detailer in Kilgore had this problem. Her texts to customers were great. Short, funny, specific. "Found a wedding ring under your seat. It is in the cup holder. You are welcome." Then she started using ChatGPT for Facebook and every post sounded like a car dealership. Engagement dropped and she figured people did not care about detailing posts. They cared. They just could not hear her anymore.

The fix is not to stop using the tool. The fix is to feed it your real language and a real thing that happened, make it write for one person, then read the result out loud and cut anything you would never say.

This comes after the content kit because the kit taught structure. This lesson teaches voice, and voice is what makes the structure worth reading.

## The lesson

### 1. Capture real source language

ChatGPT can only match a voice it has seen. So show it yours.

Collect a writing sample. Three to five texts you sent customers this month. A Facebook post you wrote yourself, before any tool. An email you typed in a hurry. Hurried writing is the most honest sample you have, because you were not performing.

Then get a source note. A source note is one real thing that happened this week, in your words. The job that ran long. The question a customer asked twice. The truck that showed up with two years of dog hair. If typing is slow, record a voice memo on your phone while you explain it to an imaginary customer, use your phone's transcription, and paste the text. Do not clean it up. The mess is the voice.

Put the writing sample and the source note together in a new chat inside your project, named for the piece. If you will use this sample often, save it as a file in the project called voice-sample-2026.txt so every future chat can use it.

What good looks like: a stranger reading the sample could describe how you talk in three sentences.

The mistake is giving ChatGPT a description of your voice instead of a sample. "Friendly but professional" describes ten million businesses. Twelve real sentences describe one.

Example: a lawn crew owner in Gladewater pastes four texts and a voice memo transcript about a sprinkler head he found with the mower. That is enough.

### 2. Write for one reader

The exact working prompt below does two things in order. First it studies your sample and describes the voice using observable traits. Not "warm." Things it can point to. "Sentences average eight words. Starts with what happened, not with a greeting. Uses 'y'all' once per message. Never uses exclamation points. Ends with a plain instruction."

Read that description before you read anything else. If it is wrong, correct it in the chat. "I do use exclamation points, about one per post." That correction improves everything after it.

Then it writes one Facebook post and one email for the audience and goal you name. Name one reader, not a crowd. Not "homeowners." "Pam in Longview, whose water heater died Tuesday, who has never called a plumber before and is worried about the price." One reader makes the writing specific. Specific reads as human.

Give it the goal the way you did in lesson two: the piece, the reader, the action. The prompt already forbids em dashes, fake quotes, invented experiences, and motivational filler, and it ends every piece with one useful next action.

Facebook and email are different rooms. A post is you talking to people who follow you, so it can start in the middle of the story. An email lands in a crowded inbox, so the subject line has to earn the open and the body has to get to the point in two lines.

What good looks like: the post could only have come from your business, and the email could only be about this week.

The mistake is writing for everyone. The moment the reader is "our valued customers," the writing goes flat. Put a name on it.

Example: the detailer names her reader "a guy in Kilgore who drives a work truck, has a dog, and thinks detailing is for show cars." The post opens with the dog hair.

### 3. Read aloud and remove generic phrasing

Now the part almost nobody does. Read both pieces out loud. Actually out loud, with your mouth. Not in your head.

Your ear catches what your eye skips. Anything you stumble on, anything that makes you feel like you are reading a script, anything you would never say to a customer standing in front of you. Those get cut or rewritten in your own words.

The usual offenders: "we pride ourselves," "look no further," "your satisfaction is our priority," "elevate," "we go above and beyond," "exciting news," "don't miss out." Also the sentence that is true but not yours, like a story detail you did not tell it. If it is not in the source note, it did not happen. Cut it.

Make the fixes yourself, or tell ChatGPT the exact line and the exact replacement. "Replace 'we go above and beyond' with 'we found the ring and put it in the cup holder.'" Then read it aloud once more.

Mark three changes. This is the assignment. Three places where a specific edit made it sound more like you. Write them down with the before and the after. Those three lines are the start of a personal do-not-say list you will use in every lesson after this one.

What good looks like: you could record the post as a video without changing a word.

The mistake is polishing until it is smooth. Smooth is generic. Your real writing has edges. Keep them.

Example: a plumber in Marshall reads his email aloud and hears "our team of experienced professionals." He has two guys. He changes it to "me and Cody." Done.

## Worked example

Redbird Detailing in Kilgore is a fictional example. Tasha runs it out of a two-bay shop. Her writing sample is four texts, including the wedding ring one. Her source note, typed fast: "Guy brought in a 2019 F-150, said it was a little dirty. Two years of lab hair in the seats. Took three hours with the extractor. Found a wedding ring under the passenger seat. Called him before I even finished. He had been looking for it since Christmas."

She opens a chat named "Ring truck post and email," pastes the sample and the note into the exact working prompt, and sets the audience and goal: "Truck owners in Kilgore and Overton who think detailing is only for show cars. Goal: get them to text a photo of their interior for a quote at 903-555-0163."

Example output:

```text
Voice traits: short sentences, mostly under ten words. Starts with the event, not a greeting. Dry humor without exclamation points. Signs off with "you are welcome." Ends with a plain instruction.
Facebook post: A guy dropped off a 2019 F-150 last week and said it was a little dirty. Two years of lab hair. Three hours with the extractor. Also found a wedding ring under the passenger seat that had been missing since Christmas. If your truck is a little dirty, text a photo of the inside to 903-555-0163 and I will send you a quote. Let us restore your ride.
Email subject: Your truck is probably not as clean as you think
```

She reads both aloud. The post is close until the last line. "Let us restore your ride" is not something she has ever said. She replaces it with "You are welcome," which is how she actually signs off. The email body had "we treat every vehicle like it is our own." Cut. It also said "our trained technicians." It is her and a part-timer. Changed to "me."

Those are her three marked changes. Review against the pass standard: source-backed details, yes, every fact came from the note; fits the channel, the post starts mid-story and the email leads with a subject line that earns the open; credible aloud, yes, after the three cuts. She saves the sample, the note, both drafts, both finals, and the three changes with before and after.

## Exact working prompt

```text
Study this writing sample and source note: [paste]. First describe the voice using observable traits. Then write one Facebook post and one email for [audience and goal]. Preserve the natural sentence rhythm and plain language. Do not use em dashes, fake quotes, invented experiences, or generic motivational filler. End with one useful next action.
```

## Guided practice

1. Collect three to five texts or posts you wrote yourself and paste them into a new project chat.
2. Type or transcribe a source note about one real thing that happened this week, and do not clean it up.
3. Run the exact working prompt with one named reader and one goal, and correct the voice description if it is off.
4. Read both pieces out loud and cut every line you would never say.
5. Write down three before-and-after changes and add them to your voice and channel checklist.

## Assignment and evidence

Create a post and email from the same source note, read both aloud, and highlight three changes that made them sound more like you.

Save the source material, the prompt, the first result, the final result, and your review notes.

## Pass standard

The writing uses source-backed details, fits the channel, and sounds credible when spoken aloud.

## Common mistakes

- Describing your voice instead of showing it. Fix: paste real texts and posts you wrote without help.
- Writing for "our customers." Fix: name one reader with one situation.
- Letting a story detail in that you never told it. Fix: if it is not in the source note, cut it.
- Reading silently. Fix: read out loud and trust your ear.
- Sanding off every edge. Fix: keep the phrases that are actually yours, even if they are odd.

## Recording plan

**Target runtime:** 5 to 8 minutes.
**Primary visual:** Source voice to channel adaptation.
**Screen recording:** Paste the texts and source note into the prompt in a project chat, show the voice traits it returns, show the post and email, read them aloud on camera, and make the three edits live.
**Course map:** the course visual below anchors where this lesson sits.

![The ChatGPT Operator course visual](/images/academy/chatgpt-operator.svg)

## Downloadable

[Open the ChatGPT Operator workbook](/downloads/operator-academy/chatgpt-operator-workbook.pdf)

Workbook section: OA01 ChatGPT Operator Workbook, Voice and channel checklist.

## Lesson check

Complete the practice, mark the assignment, and score at least 80 percent on the objective lesson check. Retakes are allowed. Major builds and capstones require reviewer approval before the completion credential can be issued.
$lesson$
from public.courses c where c.id = l.course_id and c.slug = 'chatgpt-operator' and l.slug = 'write-social-posts-and-emails-that-sound-human';

update public.lessons l set content = $lesson$# CG L06: Build a Working One Page Landing Page

## What you will finish

Use ChatGPT's working preview to build and revise a responsive one-page site with a clear offer and functional form states. When you are done you will have a one-page site you can open in a browser, tested at desktop and phone widths, with a form that shows loading, success, and error states and tells the truth about where it sends.

## Why this matters

Here is the pitch every business owner gets: "Landing pages are easy now." Then they pay a monthly fee for a page builder, drag some blocks around, and end up with a page that says four things, has a form that goes nowhere, and looks broken on a phone. The fee goes up next year.

A pressure washing company in Longview had a page like that. The form had no confirmation. People filled it out, saw nothing happen, and filled it out again, or called, or gave up. He had no idea which.

ChatGPT can build a real one-page site in its working preview. You describe the page, it writes the code, and you see the page render beside the chat. You can click the buttons. You can shrink it to phone width. You can fill out the form and watch it respond. That is a building you get to inspect before anyone walks in.

This is the second deliverable lesson. Lessons four and five gave you an image and a voice. This lesson puts an offer on one page and tests it. When you want the real thing live on your own domain, that is what /free-build is for. Here, you learn what a good page has so you can judge one.

## The lesson

### 1. Give the page one job

A landing page has one job. One offer, one action. The exact working prompt already made that decision for the practice build: a fictional company, Pine Ridge Pressure Washing in Longview, homeowners getting ready for spring, and one offer, a free driveway assessment.

Read the prompt and notice what it does. It names the audience. It names the single offer. It lists the sections in order: hero, three services, three-step process, trust section, FAQ, contact form. It names the form fields. It names every form state. It sets three colors. It requires mobile responsive and accessible. And it says the form must not claim to send anywhere unless a backend is connected.

That last line is the honesty rule, and it is the one most page builders break. A form that says "Thanks, we'll be in touch" while sending nothing is a lie to your customer. In the preview, nothing is connected, so the success message has to say so.

What good looks like: you can state the page's job in one sentence before you build it. "Get a Longview homeowner to request a free driveway assessment."

The mistake is stuffing a page with everything the business does. Five services, a gallery, a newsletter signup, a map, and a coupon. The reader does not know what to do, so they do nothing.

Example: for your own business, swap the fictional details, keep the structure, keep one offer.

### 2. Request a working responsive build

Open a new chat inside your project. Name it "Landing page build." Paste the exact working prompt. It asks for the build in the working preview, which is ChatGPT's canvas: a panel that opens beside the chat where it writes the page as a single HTML file and gives you a preview control to see it rendered.

If the code appears but the page does not, look for the preview control at the top of the canvas and switch to it. If your plan does not show a canvas, ask for the same page as one complete HTML file, copy it into a text editor, save it as index.html, and open it in your browser. Same test, one more step.

Responsive means the three services sit side by side on a wide screen and stack on a narrow one. Text stays readable without pinching. Buttons are big enough for a thumb. Accessible means the form fields have labels, the colors have enough contrast to read, and you can move through the page with the Tab key.

Read the copy it wrote. The hero should say what the offer is and who it is for in the first two lines. The trust section must be labeled as sample placeholders, not fake reviews with fake names. If it invented "Sarah M., five stars," that fails the prompt and fails your customer.

What good looks like: the page opens, it is obvious what to do, and nothing on it pretends to be real that is not.

The mistake is treating the first build as finished. It is a first draft that happens to load.

Example: a gym in Tyler runs this prompt with its own details and gets a page with a "Join now" button that scrolls nowhere. That is a test failure waiting for step three.

### 3. Test every interaction and state

Now test like a customer, not like the person who built it.

Desktop first. Scroll top to bottom. Click every button and every link. Does each one go somewhere or scroll to the form? Open the FAQ items.

Then phone width. Drag the preview narrow, or open the saved file on your phone. Do the sections stack? Is the hero text readable? Can you tap the form fields without zooming?

Then the form. Leave it empty and submit. You should see an error state that says which field is missing. Type a phone number with letters in it. Error. Fill it correctly and submit. You should see a loading state, then a success message. Read the success message. It must not claim the request was sent. "Demo form. Nothing was submitted." is honest. "We'll call you within an hour" is not, because nothing happened.

Tab through the page with the keyboard. Every field and button should get focus in order.

Write down what you found. Then make one revision, and make it evidence-based. "At phone width, the copper button text is hard to read on cream. Change the button text to the dark green." Not "make it look better." Run it, test that one thing again, and save the file with the download control on the canvas.

Save the final file, a desktop screenshot, a phone screenshot, and your test notes. That is your deliverable package.

What good looks like: you tried to break the form three ways and it told the truth every time.

The mistake is testing only the happy path. Real customers leave fields blank and type their email in the phone box. Your page has to handle that without lying.

Example: a roofer in Gilmer tests his version, finds the FAQ items do not open on his phone, and makes that his one revision.

## Worked example

Pine Ridge Pressure Washing is the fictional business written into the prompt, so the worked example uses it as is. The operator runs the prompt in a chat named "Pine Ridge landing page" inside a project set up for practice.

Example output:

```text
I built the page in the canvas as a single HTML file. Open the preview to see it rendered.
Sections: hero with the free driveway assessment offer, three services, three-step process, a trust section with three cards labeled "Sample placeholder, replace with a real review," a six-question FAQ, and the contact form.
Form: name, phone, email, service select. Empty required fields show an inline error. Submitting shows a loading state for about a second, then a success message.
Colors: deep forest green headings and buttons, warm cream background, copper accents.
Note: the form does not send anywhere. The success message says this is a demo and nothing was submitted.
```

Desktop test: everything scrolls and every button jumps to the form. Phone width: services stack correctly, but the copper text on the cream call-to-action button is faint. Form: empty submit shows "Please enter your phone number" under the right field. Bad phone shows an error. Good submit shows a spinner, then the demo message. Tab order works.

Review against the pass standard. Loads: yes. Responds to screen size: yes. Explains the fictional offer: the hero says "Free driveway assessment for Longview homeowners getting ready for spring." Labels examples: the trust cards say sample placeholder. Handles form interaction honestly: yes, the success message admits nothing was sent.

The one evidence-based revision: "The call-to-action button uses copper text on cream and is hard to read at phone width. Change the button text to deep forest green and keep everything else." Rerun, retest the button, download the file. The operator saves index.html, two screenshots, and the test notes in one folder, shares it as viewable by link, and submits.

## Exact working prompt

```text
Build a polished one-page landing page in the working preview for this fictional business: Pine Ridge Pressure Washing in Longview, Texas. Audience: homeowners preparing for spring. Offer: free driveway assessment. Include a strong hero, three services, three-step process, trust section with clearly labeled sample placeholders rather than invented reviews, FAQ, and contact form with name, phone, email, service, loading, success, and error states. Use deep forest green, warm cream, and copper. Make it mobile responsive and accessible. Do not claim the form sends anywhere unless a backend is connected.
```

## Guided practice

1. Open a new chat in your project named "Landing page build" and paste the exact working prompt as written.
2. Open the preview and scroll the whole page at desktop width, clicking every button and link.
3. Narrow the preview to phone width and check that sections stack and text stays readable.
4. Submit the form empty, with a bad phone number, and correctly, and read the success message for honesty.
5. Make one evidence-based revision, retest that one thing, and download the file with two screenshots and your notes.

## Assignment and evidence

Run the build, inspect desktop and mobile, test the form states, and make one evidence-based revision.

Save the source material, the prompt, the first result, the final result, and your review notes. This is a designated deliverable lesson. Use the submission panel below to send the viewable link for review.

## Pass standard

The page loads, responds to screen size, explains the fictional offer, labels examples, and handles form interaction honestly.

## Common mistakes

- Adding sections to the prompt until the page has five jobs. Fix: one offer, one action, and the sections listed.
- Accepting fake reviews in the trust section. Fix: every card is labeled as a sample placeholder until you have real permission.
- Testing only on a wide screen. Fix: narrow the preview and open the file on your phone.
- A success message that claims the request was sent. Fix: in a demo, say nothing was submitted.
- Asking for a general "make it better" revision. Fix: name the defect you saw, the width you saw it at, and the exact change.

## Recording plan

**Target runtime:** 5 to 8 minutes.
**Primary visual:** Prompt to preview to test to revision loop.
**Screen recording:** Paste the prompt, open the canvas preview, scroll the rendered page, narrow it to phone width, submit the form empty and then correctly, read the demo success message, and make the button contrast revision.
