update public.lessons l set content = $lesson$# AD L02: Calculate Budget Guardrails

## What you will finish

Set a test budget and stop thresholds from economics and capacity. When you are done you will have a one-page budget sheet with a conservative, base, and strong case, a daily cap, a total cap, and stop rules written down before the first dollar goes out.

## Why this matters

Most owners set an ad budget the way they set a thermostat. Whatever feels comfortable. A gym in Longview spends $900 in a month "to see what happens," and at the end of the month nobody can say what came back. There was no rule to stop, so nobody stopped.

I made the opposite mistake with my own money. I turned off an ad after nineteen cents of spend. Six impressions. Four people reached. It had not failed. It had not run. Both mistakes come from the same place: no rule written down before the money moved.

The budget has to come from what a job is worth to you, not from what feels safe. A water heater install and a $40 oil change cannot afford the same lead. Your sheet says what you can pay, what you will spend to find out, and when you stop.

Bring the campaign brief from lesson one. The offer and the capacity number on it are the inputs here, and the caps you set now are what lesson seven enforces and lesson eight reads against.

## The lesson

### 1. Estimate contribution margin

Start with the average sale for the one offer on your brief. Pull the last ten invoices for that job and average them. Not your best job. The average.

Then figure what is left after the job is done. Parts, the labor you pay someone else, fuel, the disposal fee, the permit. That is contribution. It is not net profit after your salary and the shop rent. It is the money one more job adds to the pile.

Say the water heater job averages $1,800. Materials $700, tech labor $350, fuel and permit $50. Contribution is $700. Every number in this lesson is an example like that one. Yours will be different, and that is the point of doing it.

Now close rate. Of the qualified leads for this job, how many become a sale? Look at last month's calls if you logged them. If you did not, and most people did not, use three cases: conservative one in five, base one in three, strong one in two. Never one number. One number is a guess dressed up as a fact.

What good looks like: the average sale, the contribution, and three close rates, each with a note that says "from invoices," "from last month's calls," or "assumption."

The mistake: using revenue. An $1,800 sale is not $1,800 you can spend to get it. I watch owners run that math and wonder why the month ended in the red with a full calendar.

### 2. Choose an affordable acquisition range

Break-even lead cost is contribution times close rate. In the example, $700 times one in three is about $233. That is the most you could pay for one qualified lead and make exactly nothing. Run it for all three cases: $140 conservative, $233 base, $350 strong.

Your target is a fraction of break-even, because the campaign has to leave money for the business. I use a third as a starting point. The base case target lands around $78 per qualified lead.

Now hear this part clearly. Those numbers describe what you can afford. They do not predict what Meta will charge you. Nobody knows that until you run. I will not tell you your cost per lead, and anyone who tells you theirs as a promise is selling something. The sheet is an affordability check, not a forecast.

Add payback. If you get paid when the job is done, payback is days and the test budget only has to float a week or two. If you invoice net 30, the test budget has to cover a month before anything comes back. Write which one you are.

Then capacity, from the brief. Six installs a week at a one in three close is about 18 qualified leads a week. Past that you are paying for leads you cannot serve, and a lead you cannot serve turns into a one star review. Cap it there.

If you want to see what one missed lead costs in this offer, run the missed call calculator at /tools/missed-call-calculator. There are 86 free calculators at /tools. The missed call number belongs on the sheet, because follow-up speed changes close rate more than any ad setting does.

What good looks like: three break-even numbers, three targets, a payback line, and a weekly lead ceiling from capacity.

The mistake: treating the strong case as the plan. Plan on conservative. Be glad when base shows up.

### 3. Cap the learning test

Two caps and a set of stop rules. Daily cap and total cap.

The daily cap comes from the learning read. In my account, the first honest look at an offer needs 48 to 72 hours of delivery, or roughly $70 to $105 of spend, before I change anything structural. So daily budget times three days is the minimum you will spend to learn anything at all. At $35 a day that is $105. If that number scares you, run fewer days at the same daily budget, not more days at $5.

Do not split it. Eight ad sets at $5 a day is $40 a day that teaches you nothing, because none of them ever gets enough delivery to read. One ad set, one budget.

The total cap for the test: enough to buy roughly five to ten qualified leads at your target cost, or two weeks, whichever comes first. In the example, $500. If the target is wrong, and it might be, $500 tells you that without hurting the business.

Then the stop rules, written as sentences a spouse could enforce:

Stop when spend reaches $500 with fewer than three qualified leads.
Do not change anything before 72 hours unless something is broken, like a wrong link or a wrong price.
Pause if 40 people click and nobody completes the form. That is a form problem, not a spend problem.
Do not raise the daily budget more than 20 percent in a week.

The nineteen cent ad broke the second rule. Turning it off was not a decision. It was a reflex, and it cost me the only untested creative I had that week. Write the rule so you cannot flinch.

Put all of it on one page. Assumptions at the top, the math in the middle, caps and stop rules at the bottom. Every number in the middle points at an assumption at the top. That is the pass standard, and it is what lets you fix the sheet in week two instead of arguing about it.

What good looks like: one page, and a stranger could find where every number came from in under a minute.

The mistake: "let's see how it goes" with no number, or its cousin, moving the budget every morning.

## Worked example

Rabbit Creek Plumbing in Kilgore is a fictional example. Marisol pulled ten water heater invoices and counted last month's water heater calls before she opened the prompt.

Her inputs: average sale $1,780. Materials and paid labor $1,090, so contribution $690. Last month 4 of 11 qualified calls became installs, so base case one in three, conservative one in five, strong one in two. Capacity six installs a week. Paid on completion. Test budget available $500.

She ran the exact working prompt below with "water heater replacement" in the bracket and those numbers pasted above it.

Example output:

```text
Assumptions: average sale $1,780; contribution $690; close rate 20 / 33 / 50 percent; capacity 6 installs per week; payback at completion.
Break-even qualified lead cost: $138 conservative, $230 base, $345 strong.
Target qualified lead cost (one third of break-even): $46 / $77 / $115.
Daily cap: $35. Minimum learning spend: $105 over 72 hours.
Total cap: $500 or 14 days, whichever comes first.
Stop rules: stop at $500 with under 3 qualified leads; pause at 40 clicks and 0 form completions; no structural change before 72 hours.
Expected leads at $500: about 11.
```

Review against the pass standard. Does every number trace to an assumption? Yes, until the last line. "Expected leads: about 11" divides the total cap by the conservative target and calls it a result. That is a forecast wearing a plain shirt, and it uses the lowest number on the sheet to do it. Does spending have a fixed cap and stop rule? Yes, both.

The one revision: the last line became "At the base target, $500 buys about six qualified leads. That is an affordability check, not a prediction. Actual cost is unknown until the test runs." Marisol also wrote next to the strong case: "not for planning until it shows up in real data."

## Exact working prompt

```text
Build a budget model for [offer] using average sale, gross margin, close rate, capacity, target payback, and available test budget. Show assumptions, break-even lead cost, target lead cost, daily cap, total cap, and stop rules. Do not claim forecasts are guaranteed.
```

## Guided practice

1. Pull the last ten invoices for the offer on your brief and write the average sale and the contribution.
2. Write three close rates, conservative, base, and strong, and note which one has real data behind it.
3. Multiply contribution by each close rate for three break-even lead costs, then take a third of each for targets.
4. Set a daily cap and a total cap, and check that daily cap times three lands near $70 to $105.
5. Write four stop rules as plain sentences, then run the exact prompt with everything pasted in.

## Assignment and evidence

Complete the budget sheet with conservative, base, and strong cases.

Save the source material, the prompt, the first result, the final result, and your review notes.

## Pass standard

Every budget number traces to an assumption and spending has a fixed cap and stop rule.

## Common mistakes

- Using revenue instead of contribution. Fix: subtract parts, paid labor, fuel, and permits before you do anything else.
- One close rate. Fix: three cases, and label which one has data.
- Splitting a small budget across many ad sets. Fix: one ad set, one budget, until it has taught you something.
- No stop rule, or a stop rule nobody wrote down. Fix: sentences on the sheet, with numbers, before launch.
- Treating the sheet as a forecast. Fix: label it an affordability check and let the campaign supply the real cost.

## Recording plan

**Target runtime:** 5 to 8 minutes.
**Primary visual:** Economics to spend waterfall.
**Screen recording:** A Google Sheet with the three cases side by side, typing the contribution and close rate cells and watching break-even, target, daily cap, and total cap fill in, then typing the four stop rules under them.
**Course map:** the course visual below anchors where this lesson sits.

![The Local Ads Operator course visual](/images/academy/local-ads-operator.svg)

## Downloadable

[Open the Local Ads Operator workbook](/downloads/operator-academy/local-ads-operator-workbook.pdf)

Workbook section: OA08 Ads Workbook, Budget guardrails.

## Lesson check

Complete the practice, mark the assignment, and score at least 80 percent on the objective lesson check. Retakes are allowed. Major builds and capstones require reviewer approval before the completion credential can be issued.
$lesson$
from public.courses c where c.id = l.course_id and c.slug = 'local-ads-operator' and l.slug = 'calculate-budget-guardrails';

update public.lessons l set content = $lesson$# AD L03: Build the Message Matrix

## What you will finish

Create distinct ad angles from real problems, triggers, proof, and objections. When you are done you will have a five-row matrix in your workbook, three angles picked from it, and one ad brief for each that a person with a phone could shoot from tomorrow.

## Why this matters

Most local ads say the same thing. Family owned. Licensed and insured. Call today. That is not an angle. That is a business card with a budget behind it.

An angle is the reason a person stops scrolling. My best ad opened with a question about the viewer: what do you pay every month for the tools that run your business? My weakest opened with a list of things I had built. Good footage, clean sound, five proof cards, and it ran at more than five times the cost per click. It was about me. The winner was about them.

Here is the other reason. If you run five ads that are the same idea in five fonts and one wins, you learned nothing. If you run five different ideas and one wins, you learned what your buyers respond to. That is worth more than the leads it produced.

Bring the brief from lesson one. The offer on it is the thing every angle has to sell. The proof column you build here is what lesson four is not allowed to shoot without.

## The lesson

### 1. Collect buyer language

You do not write the angles. Your customers already did. Your job is to go find what they said.

Open your call log. If you use Quo or any business phone that records, read the transcripts of the last ten calls about this offer. Open your Google reviews, and your two biggest competitors' reviews, especially the one star ones. Scroll the last twenty texts from customers. Sit in the community Facebook group for your town for fifteen minutes.

Write down exact phrases. Not the gist. The words. "It went out again." "Y'all come out to Hallsville?" "How soon can somebody get here." "I called three places and nobody called back." "Is that the real price or is there going to be more."

Then the triggers. The moment somebody starts looking. The cold snap. The pilot light. Water on the garage floor. Hail on the news. The kid's first cavity. A wedding in six weeks. The trigger is where the ad meets them.

What good looks like: twenty to thirty raw lines, each labeled with where it came from, and five triggers. Then paste the lines into ChatGPT or Claude with the prompt. Do not ask it to invent buyer language. It will, and it will sound like every other ad in the feed.

The mistake: writing what you would say about yourself. Nobody is scrolling Facebook hoping to learn that you are licensed.

Example: a dentist in Tyler reads her last month of reviews and finds the same line four times: "they did not make me feel bad about how long it had been." That is an angle. It was sitting in her reviews the whole time.

### 2. Separate angle from format

An angle is the idea. The missed call. The monthly rent. The 9 PM Friday when nobody answers. The old tank in the garage that is twelve years old.

A format is how it is delivered. A phone video. A still picture. A carousel. A text-only Search ad. Keep them in separate columns, because you will test them separately.

I learned this the expensive way. Two of my four ads were video, and they sat at opposite ends of the results table. "Video wins" was the wrong read. The video that won had motion through a real place people recognize, other people on camera, and a question in the first three seconds. The video that lost was me talking against a lake. Same format. Different angle, different execution.

So the matrix has rows for angles and columns for the parts. Buyer trigger. Problem. Promise. Proof available. Limitation. Hook. Body. Call to action. Visual idea. Five rows, and each row has to be materially different: a different trigger or a different objection, not the same promise with a new adjective.

Example, five for a plumber. One, the cold shower this morning. Two, the "nobody called me back" objection. Three, the price question answered before they ask it. Four, the twelve year old tank that has not failed yet. Five, the landlord with three rentals who wants one number to call.

What good looks like: read the trigger column top to bottom and every line is a different moment.

The mistake: five rows that all say "we are fast." If two rows share a trigger, merge them and go find another.

### 3. Match claims to proof

Every promise in the matrix needs a proof you can put on a screen. A photo of your truck at a real job, with the homeowner's OK. A review shown the way the reviewer wrote it, with the name they used. Your license number. A count you can back with invoices. "We replaced 140 water heaters last year" is a claim only if the invoices say 140.

If you cannot prove it, cut the claim and keep the feeling. I hold to that on every ad I write for a client. The emotional line stays. The figure goes unless it is documented.

The limitation column matters more than people think. What the offer does not include. Commercial, not this offer. Tankless, quoted separately. Outside the area, no. Putting the boundary in the ad keeps the wrong people from clicking, and you pay for every click.

Some things never go in the matrix. No before and after claims unless verified and allowed. No promises of results. No "cheapest in town" unless you priced every competitor this week. Nothing about a person's health, credit, or housing situation, which Meta's special ad categories cover. If your business touches health, finance, or housing, have your attorney review the claims once and move on.

One note for Google. The same matrix feeds a Search campaign. The hook becomes a headline. The proof becomes the review extension. The limitation becomes a negative keyword. Build the matrix once and both platforms eat from it.

Then pick three. Not the three you like. The three with the strongest trigger and the proof already in hand. Write one ad brief per angle: the row from the matrix, the proof file named, the format you will shoot first. That is what you carry into lesson four.

What good looks like: every promise in the three briefs has a file name next to it.

The mistake: keeping a claim because it sounds good. Sounds good is not a proof file.

## Worked example

Rabbit Creek Plumbing in Kilgore is a fictional example. Marisol pulled 24 lines from Quo transcripts, her Google reviews, and a competitor's one star reviews. A few of them: "I called at 6 and nobody picked up." "Is that the real price." "The last guy said Tuesday and showed up Friday." "It's making that noise again." "Do y'all do Overton."

She pasted all 24 lines and the offer from her brief above the prompt.

"Create a message matrix for water heater replacement, quote by phone within 24 hours, Kilgore and 20 miles. Produce five angles. For each include buyer trigger, problem, promise, proof available, limitation, hook, body, call to action, and visual idea. Remove claims that lack evidence."

Example output:

```text
Angle 2. Trigger: called a plumber after hours, no answer. Problem: they call the next name on the list. Promise: a real person calls you back within the hour, 7 to 7. Proof: office callback log, last 30 days. Limitation: after 7 PM, first thing next morning. Hook: "Who called you back last time your water heater quit?" Visual: office phone ringing, Marisol picking up.
Angle 3. Trigger: worried the quote will grow once the truck arrives. Problem: surprise charges. Promise: lowest price in Kilgore. Proof: none on file. Limitation: none listed. Hook: "Lowest price in Kilgore." Visual: price tag.
```

Review against the pass standard. Are the angles materially different? Yes. Angle 2 is about being reached and angle 3 is about price, and they come from different triggers. Is every claim supportable? Angle 3 is not. "Lowest price in Kilgore" has no proof, Marisol has not priced her competitors, and the prompt was supposed to remove it.

The one revision: angle 3's promise became "the price we say on the phone is the price on the invoice," which her office backs with a written policy and the last 30 invoices. The hook became "Is that the real price? Yes. Here is how." The limitation column got filled in: "excludes code repairs found on site, quoted before any work starts." Angles 1, 2, and 3 became her three ad briefs.

## Exact working prompt

```text
Create a message matrix for [offer]. Produce five angles. For each include buyer trigger, problem, promise, proof available, limitation, hook, body, call to action, and visual idea. Remove claims that lack evidence.
```

## Guided practice

1. Read ten call transcripts or reviews and write twenty exact phrases with their source next to each.
2. Write five triggers, the moments your buyer starts looking.
3. Run the exact prompt with the phrases and your offer pasted above it.
4. Go down the proof column and strike every claim you cannot put on a screen.
5. Pick the three angles with the strongest trigger and proof in hand, and write one ad brief for each.

## Assignment and evidence

Select three angles and create one ad brief for each.

Save the source material, the prompt, the first result, the final result, and your review notes. This is a designated deliverable lesson. Use the submission panel below to send the viewable link for review.

## Pass standard

Each angle is materially different and every claim is supportable.

## Common mistakes

- Writing angles from your own head. Fix: twenty exact customer phrases first, then the prompt.
- Five angles with one trigger. Fix: merge duplicates and find a different moment.
- Confusing format with angle. Fix: separate columns, and test angles inside one format first.
- Keeping a claim because it sounds good. Fix: no proof file, no claim. Keep the feeling, drop the figure.
- Skipping the limitation column. Fix: write what the offer does not include so the wrong people do not click.

## Recording plan

**Target runtime:** 5 to 8 minutes.
