update public.lessons l set content = $lesson$# OE L01: Choose One Buyer

## What you will finish

Name a specific buyer whose problem you understand well enough to solve. When you are done you will have a one-page buyer brief on the workbook Buyer page, and three real names written under it, people or businesses you could text this week.

## Why this matters

Most owners I meet in East Texas have a website that says "we do it all." Mowing, landscaping, irrigation, fence repair, Christmas lights. Bookkeeping, payroll, taxes, "and more." It feels safe. It is not.

Here is the scene. The phone rings while you are under a house or on a roof. It is someone asking for the one thing you barely do. You quote it anyway because money is money. You win it at a bad price, it eats a week, and the good call, the one you were built for, went to voicemail and called the next guy.

An offer for everyone gets priced for no one. You cannot define a result, write a scope, or set a price until you know who is on the other end. That is why this lesson comes first. Every page after this one starts with the buyer you name today.

This is not about turning work away forever. It is about deciding who your best offer is built for and saying so out loud. Take the odd job if you want. Just stop building the business around it.

## The lesson

If you took The ChatGPT Operator, you know how to run a prompt. If you did not, here is everything you need for this course: open ChatGPT or Claude, paste the exact working prompt, replace the bracket with your own words, and answer its questions with facts. That is it.

### 1. Start with lived evidence

Open the workbook Buyer page or a blank Google Doc. Write down your last ten paid jobs. Real ones. Name, town, what they bought, what they paid, how long it took, and one line about how it went.

Now circle the three you would take again tomorrow. Not the biggest. The ones that paid on time, did not fight the invoice, and told somebody about you.

What good looks like: you can see a pattern in the circles. Same kind of person, same kind of problem, same reason they called. Maybe it is the property manager with a vendor who keeps skipping weeks. Maybe it is the contractor whose books are six months behind.

The mistake: starting with who you wish would buy. "High-end clients." "Bigger companies." Wishes are not evidence. Your bank deposits are evidence. Your call log is evidence. The jobs you dread are evidence too, because they tell you who the offer is not for.

Example: Big Pine Roofing in Henderson is a fictional example. The owner lists forty jobs and circles three. All homeowners with a roof over fifteen years old. All called after the same hail event. All had an adjuster already scheduled. That is a buyer. "Anyone who needs a roof" was never a buyer.

### 2. Name the costly problem

A buyer is a person with a problem that costs them something they can feel. Money, time, sleep, embarrassment in front of a board. Write the problem in the buyer's words, not yours.

Here is the test. Say the sentence to that person's face. If they would nod, you have it. If they would say "well, kind of," keep digging.

What good looks like: one sentence, no jargon. "The mow crew skips weeks and the residents complain to me." "The CPA sends me forty questions every April because my books are a mess." "My phone rings while I am under a house and I never call them back."

Put a number next to it when you honestly can. Not a promise. A cost. If you miss calls, run your own numbers through /tools/missed-call-calculator. It shows what a missed call is worth to you, and the same math works for your buyer.

The mistake: naming a problem the buyer does not feel yet. "Their brand is inconsistent." Nobody in Kilgore lies awake over brand consistency. They lie awake over the payroll deposit on Friday.

Example: Reyes Bookkeeping in Kilgore, fictional. Marisol started with "small businesses that need bookkeeping." After listing her last ten clients, she saw that the best three were all plumbing and HVAC owners doing their own books at the kitchen table. The costly problem, in their words: "I cannot tell which jobs made money, and every April is a fire drill." That sentence is the whole offer, pointed the right way.

### 3. Exclude poor-fit buyers

This is the part owners skip because it feels rude. It is not rude. It is honest, and it saves both of you a bad month.

Write a short "not for" list. Who calls you that you should send somewhere else? Who costs you money every time? Who wants a result you cannot deliver?

What good looks like: three to five lines a stranger could apply without asking you. "Not for one-time cleanups." "Not for businesses more than three years behind on their books." "Not for anyone choosing on the lowest bid."

The mistake: making the list so soft it excludes nobody. "Not for people who are not serious" is not a line. It is a mood.

Example: Pine Curve Lawn and Landscape in Gilmer, fictional, and you will see them in every lesson of this course. Dale wrote: not for homeowners wanting a one-time cleanup, not for apartment complexes that need a daily crew, not for boards that pick the cheapest bid every year. He said it felt like turning down money. Then he looked at his ten-job list and saw those were exactly the jobs that lost money.

Now run the exact working prompt. It asks five questions one at a time. Answer from your ten-job list, not from memory, and if it asks something you do not know, say "I do not know" instead of guessing. When it returns the brief, read it against the pass standard: specific, observable, reachable, and connected to a problem you can honestly help solve. If the buyer description could fit half the county, answer the questions again with more detail.

## Worked example

Pine Curve Lawn and Landscape, Gilmer. Fictional. Two crews, one trailer, and a website that lists eleven services.

Input: Dale's last ten paid jobs. Six residential mows, one sod install, one set of Christmas lights, and three HOA common-area contracts. The three HOAs paid on time, renewed, and one of them referred him. Two of the residential jobs ended in arguments over the invoice.

He opened ChatGPT and pasted the exact working prompt with the bracket replaced by "common-area lawn maintenance for HOAs and small office parks." It asked five questions, one at a time: who paid you most recently and why, what did they try before you, what happened right before they called, what did they complain about, and who have you turned away.

Example output:

```text
Buyer description: Volunteer HOA board presidents and part-time property managers responsible for common areas in 20 to 80 home neighborhoods within 25 miles of Gilmer.
Urgent problem: The current crew skips weeks, residents complain at meetings, and the president has to chase the vendor around a day job.
Failed alternatives: Lowest bid from a Facebook post, a regional company that treats them as a small account, a resident's nephew.
Buying trigger: A missed week before a neighborhood event, or the second no-show from the current vendor.
Not for: Homeowners wanting a one-time cleanup, complexes needing daily crews, boards that choose on price alone.
```

Review against the pass standard. Specific: yes, a board president with a day job. Observable: yes, you can drive the neighborhood and see the common area. Reachable: yes, boards post meeting notices and management companies list contacts. Connected to a problem Dale can honestly solve: yes, he can show up on schedule and prove it with photos.

The one revision: the first draft said "within 60 miles of Gilmer." Dale's crews lose an hour each way past 25. He cut the radius, and the buyer got more real, not less. Then he wrote three names under the brief: the two boards he already mows for, and the office park on Highway 271 whose grass he drives past every morning and has never once called.

## Exact working prompt

```text
Act as an offer strategist. Help me define one specific buyer for [skill/service]. Ask five questions one at a time, then return: buyer description, urgent problem, failed alternatives, buying trigger, and who this is not for. Do not invent facts.
```

## Guided practice

1. List your last ten paid jobs with name, town, what they bought, what they paid, and one line on how it went.
2. Circle the three you would take again tomorrow and write what they have in common.
3. Paste the exact working prompt into ChatGPT or Claude, replace the bracket, and answer the five questions from your list.
4. Copy the result onto the workbook Buyer page and rewrite any line that could describe half the county.
5. Write three real names or businesses under the brief that fit it.

## Assignment and evidence

Complete a one-page ideal buyer brief and name three people or businesses that fit it.

Save the source material, the prompt, the first result, the final result, and your review notes.

## Pass standard

The buyer is specific, observable, reachable, and connected to a problem you can honestly help solve.

## Common mistakes

- Picking the buyer you wish you had instead of the one who already paid. Fix: start from the ten-job list every time.
- Describing a category instead of a person. Fix: "small businesses" is a category; "the owner doing books at the kitchen table at 10 pm" is a person.
- Letting ChatGPT fill in facts you did not give it. Fix: if a line in the brief is not something you saw or heard, delete it.
- A "not for" list that excludes nobody. Fix: name the last three jobs that lost money and write the line that would have turned them away.
- Stopping before the three names. Fix: the brief is not done until you can text somebody who fits it.

## Recording plan

**Target runtime:** 5 to 8 minutes.
**Primary visual:** Buyer to problem fit map.
**Screen recording:** Show ChatGPT asking the five questions one at a time with the Pine Curve answers typed in, then the returned brief pasted onto the workbook Buyer page with the three names added under it.
**Course map:** the course visual below anchors where this lesson sits.

![The Offer Engine course visual](/images/academy/offer-engine.svg)

## Downloadable

[Open the Offer Engine workbook](/downloads/operator-academy/offer-engine-workbook.pdf)

Workbook section: OA03 Offer Builder Workbook, Buyer page.

## Lesson check

Complete the practice, mark the assignment, and score at least 80 percent on the objective lesson check. Retakes are allowed. Major builds and capstones require reviewer approval before the completion credential can be issued.
$lesson$
from public.courses c where c.id = l.course_id and c.slug = 'offer-engine' and l.slug = 'choose-the-buyer';

update public.lessons l set content = $lesson$# OE L02: Define the Result

## What you will finish

State a useful outcome without promising revenue, rankings, virality, or certainty. When you are done you will have one result statement and one limitations statement on the workbook Result page, both plain enough to read to a buyer over the phone without clearing your throat.

## Why this matters

A coach in Longview, fictional, promised a client "double your revenue in 90 days." The client worked hard. Revenue went up about fifteen percent. He asked for a refund, because that is what he was promised. The coach did good work and still lost, because the promise was the wrong thing to promise.

The opposite failure is just as common. A roofer's page says "quality you can trust." The homeowner reads it and still does not know what shows up in the driveway, when, or what happens when it rains. Vague results turn into scope fights later, because nobody agreed on what done means.

This lesson sits between the buyer and the scope on purpose. Bring the buyer brief from lesson one. You are going to write what will exist for that buyer when you are finished, in words that are true on your worst day, not your best one.

Next lesson turns that result into a scope card. You cannot package a fog.

## The lesson

Here is the rule I use. Promise the thing you control. Describe the thing you hope for. Never mix the two in one sentence.

### 1. Separate output from outcome

An output is what you make. A mowed common area, a reconciled set of books, a new roof, a live website. An outcome is what the buyer hopes happens next. Fewer complaints, a calm April, more calls, a dry living room.

You control the output. You influence the outcome. The buyer buys because of the outcome, so you have to talk about it. But you only promise the output.

How to do it: draw two columns on the Result page. Left, what will exist when you are done. Right, what the buyer hopes changes because of it. Write the offer from the left column. Speak about the right column with the words "so that" and "built to."

What good looks like: "You will have a 12-month maintenance schedule, a completed first visit with dated photos, and one number to text, so that you stop chasing your vendor." The first half is promised. The second half is why it matters.

The mistake: putting the outcome in the promise. "We will stop the complaints." You cannot. Residents complain about things you do not control, including the neighbor's dog.

Example: Keisha Bell, a realtor in Tyler, fictional. Output: professional photos, a staging checklist, a pricing comparison from the last six months of nearby sales, and the listing live within seven days. Outcome she hopes for: sold in thirty days. She promises the first list. She works toward the second and says so.

### 2. Bound the time and scope

A result without an edge is a trap. "Ongoing support" means forever. "We handle your books" means everything. Put a fence around it.

How to do it: answer three questions inside the result statement. When does it start and when does it end? What is inside the fence? What is outside it? Use a number for time. Thirty days, six weeks, one quarter.

What good looks like: "Within 30 days of signing" beats "quickly." "Mowing, edging, trimming, and bed weeding" beats "lawn care." "Does not include tree removal or irrigation repair" beats silence.

The mistake: leaving the edges to be discussed later. Later is when the board president asks about the ditch by the entrance and you have already quoted the price.

Example: Reyes Bookkeeping, Kilgore, fictional. Marisol's first draft: "I get your books in order." Bounded: "In 90 days you will have twelve months of reconciled books, a chart of accounts that matches how you bid jobs, and a one-page monthly close checklist your office manager can run. Catch-up beyond twelve months is quoted separately." A plumber can picture that. He cannot picture "in order."

### 3. Remove unsupported promises

Now hunt the draft for words you cannot back. Revenue. Rankings. Leads. Best. Painless. In no time. Any number about the buyer's business that you did not measure.

How to do it: read every sentence and ask, "If this does not happen, does the buyer get to say I lied?" If yes, cut the word or move it to the "so that" side with a softer verb.

Then write the limitations statement. Two to four sentences, plain language, that say what you do not control. This is not a legal disclaimer. It is the honest paragraph that makes the rest of the page believable. If your work touches contracts or consent, have your attorney review the wording once and move on.

What good looks like: "I control the schedule, the crew, and the photos. I do not control the weather, your residents, or the board vote. Rain days are made up within three business days."

The mistake: believing limits scare buyers off. The buyer who has been burned before, which is most of your buyer brief, is looking for the person who says what they will not do. The hype is what scares them.

Example: Sabine Strength, a gym in Longview, fictional. The first draft of a six-week intro program said "lose 15 pounds." Cut. Final: "Six coached sessions, a written program you keep, and a form check on the three main lifts. What you do with it after week six is up to you." That is a promise a gym can keep on a bad week.

Now run the prompt. Paste your broadest service line into the bracket, the one off your website or your truck. It returns five bounded statements. Pick the one that fits your buyer brief, then rewrite it in your own words. The version you would say out loud is the version that goes on the page.

## Worked example

Pine Curve Lawn and Landscape, Gilmer. Fictional. Bring the buyer brief from lesson one: HOA board presidents and part-time property managers with a mow vendor who keeps skipping weeks.

Dale's broad service line, straight off his website: "Full-service lawn care and landscaping for residential and commercial properties."

He pasted the exact working prompt into ChatGPT with the bracket replaced by that sentence. Five statements came back. This is the one that matched the brief.

Example output:

```text
For: HOA boards and property managers with common areas of up to three acres.
Will finish: Within 30 days of signing, a written 12-month maintenance schedule, your first two weekly service visits completed with dated photos sent to one contact, and one phone line with next-business-day response.
Included: Mowing, edging, string trimming, bed weeding, and blowing on every scheduled visit.
Not promised: Fewer resident complaints, board renewal, or any change in property values. Weather delays are rescheduled, not skipped.
You will finally have peace of mind about your common areas.
```

Review against the pass standard. Can a buyer tell what will exist at the end? Yes: a schedule, two completed visits with photos, a phone line. Does the language avoid promising business results? Almost. The last line promises peace of mind, and Dale cannot deliver a feeling.

The one revision: he cut the last line and wrote the limitations statement under the result instead. "I control the schedule, the crew, and the photos. I do not control the weather, your residents, or the board vote. Rain days are made up within three business days."

Then he read both statements to a board president he already mows for. She said, "So if it rains Tuesday you come Thursday?" That question told him the statement worked. She was already picturing it.

## Exact working prompt

```text
Rewrite this broad service into five bounded outcome statements: [service]. Each must say who it is for, what they will finish, what is included, and what is not guaranteed.
```

## Guided practice

1. Copy your broadest service sentence from your website or Facebook page onto the workbook Result page.
2. Draw two columns, output and outcome, and sort every claim you make into one of them.
3. Paste the exact working prompt into ChatGPT or Claude with your service in the bracket, and pick the one statement that fits your buyer brief.
4. Rewrite it in your own words with a time limit, what is included, and what is not.
5. Write the limitations statement, then read both aloud to one person who fits the buyer brief.

## Assignment and evidence

Write one final result statement and a plain-language limitations statement.

Save the source material, the prompt, the first result, the final result, and your review notes.

## Pass standard

A buyer can tell what will exist at the end, while the language avoids guaranteed business results.

## Common mistakes

- Promising the outcome instead of the output. Fix: if you cannot make it happen with your own hands, it goes after "so that."
- No time boundary. Fix: put a number of days or weeks in the first sentence.
- Softening the limits into legal mush. Fix: write them the way you would say them across a truck hood.
- Keeping "best," "painless," or "in no time" because it sounds good. Fix: cut it, or replace it with something you can show.
- Letting the five versions replace your own voice. Fix: pick one, then rewrite it out loud.

## Recording plan

**Target runtime:** 5 to 8 minutes.
**Primary visual:** Output versus outcome chart.
**Screen recording:** Show the broad service sentence on the left and the five bounded statements on the right, then strike "peace of mind" out of the chosen one live and type the limitations statement under it on the Result page.
**Course map:** the course visual below anchors where this lesson sits.

![The Offer Engine course visual](/images/academy/offer-engine.svg)

## Downloadable

[Open the Offer Engine workbook](/downloads/operator-academy/offer-engine-workbook.pdf)

Workbook section: OA03 Offer Builder Workbook, Result page.

## Lesson check

Complete the practice, mark the assignment, and score at least 80 percent on the objective lesson check. Retakes are allowed. Major builds and capstones require reviewer approval before the completion credential can be issued.
$lesson$
