update public.lessons l set content = $lesson$# OE L04: Build the Proof Plan

## What you will finish

Identify ethical proof that reduces buyer uncertainty. When you are done you will have a proof inventory with a source link on every line, one unsupported claim deleted from your public materials, and the next proof asset you are going to make, named and dated.

## Why this matters

I was reading a roofer's website in Henderson. Top of the page: "#1 roofer in East Texas." I asked the owner, according to who? He laughed. A web guy typed it in 2019. Nobody had checked it since, and nobody had believed it either.

Here is what a buyer does with a claim. They read it, and they ask, quietly, "says who?" If the page cannot answer, the claim costs you trust instead of earning it. And the buyer who has been burned before, which is most of the people in your buyer brief, is scanning for the first thing that smells like a stretch.

Proof is not star ratings and "great to work with." Proof is anything a stranger can check. A count. A date. A photo with a date on it. A document. A named client who agreed to be named. That is why this lesson comes before price. What you can prove changes what you can ask, and how much risk you have to carry.

Bring the scope card from lesson three. Every claim you make in public should trace back to something on that card or to a fact you can point at.

## The lesson

### 1. Inventory existing evidence

Most owners have more proof than they think and less than they claim. Start by collecting what already exists.

How to do it: open the workbook Proof inventory page or a spreadsheet. Four columns: claim, evidence, source link or file, label. Now walk your phone, your invoices, your Google Business Profile, your job photo folder, your certificates, your insurance. Every fact about your business goes on a line.

What good looks like: "In business since 2019. Evidence: state filing and first invoice. Link." "Fourteen properties under contract. Evidence: signed agreements in Drive. Link." "General liability insurance. Evidence: current certificate. File." "Photo of a common area, June 12. Evidence: dated photo, board permission by email. Link."

The mistake: only counting testimonials. A count of jobs with dates is stronger than a compliment. And permission matters. Ask before you name a client or show their property. Get it in writing, even a text, and save the text as the source.

Example: my own site, RealRyanNichols.com, has a records archive with more than 1,500 entries. I can say that because the number is on the site and you can count. I cannot say "one of the largest archives in Texas," because I never measured the others. Same fact, two claims, one of them provable.

### 2. Separate facts from claims

Now label every line. Verified: you can show it right now. Supported but incomplete: true as far as you know, but the evidence is partial or the wording is bigger than the evidence. Unsupported: you cannot show it.

How to do it: read each claim the way a skeptical buyer would. Say "prove it" out loud. If the proof is a link or a file, verified. If the proof is "I have been doing this a long time," incomplete. If the proof is a feeling, unsupported.

What good looks like: most public claims land in verified, a few in incomplete with a fix written next to them, and every unsupported claim marked for deletion. Best, top, leading, painless, proven, fastest, and any number about a client's results you did not measure go straight to unsupported unless you are holding a document.

The mistake: rewriting an unsupported claim to sound softer instead of removing it. "One of the best" is not more honest than "the best." It is the same claim wearing a hedge.

Example: Sabine Strength, Longview, fictional. "Members lose 20 pounds in six weeks." Unsupported, no records, and a health claim a gym should not be making anyway. Removed. Replaced with the scope card: "Six coached sessions, a written program, a form check on three lifts." Whitfield Family Dental in Marshall, fictional, had "painless dentistry" on the door. Removed. Replaced with "same-week appointments for new patients," which the front desk can show on the calendar.

### 3. Plan the next proof asset

For every gap, choose the smallest honest asset that closes it. Small is the point. You are not producing a case study video this week. You are getting one dated photo, one permission email, one sample document with the name blurred.

How to do it: look at the supported-but-incomplete lines first, because they are the cheapest to fix. Then pick the one gap where the buyer's uncertainty is highest. Usually it is "will this actually get done" or "what does the finished thing look like." Make the asset that answers that. Put a due date on the calendar.

What good looks like: "Sample monthly walk-through report from a current property, name blurred, board permission by email. Due Friday." "Before and after of one reconciled month, account numbers blurred, client permission by text. Due Tuesday." Specific, permissioned, small.

The mistake: reaching for a testimonial you have to ask for and then wait on. Make the thing you can make alone first. A finished sample of your own work is proof that you finish.

If a tool is part of your offer, the tool itself is proof. That is why the calculators at /tools are free on my site. A buyer can use one before we ever talk, and it tells them more than a paragraph about me would.

Example: Big Pine Roofing, Henderson, fictional. Gap: homeowners cannot tell whether the crew cleans up. Smallest asset: a twenty-second phone video of the magnet sweep for nails at the end of a job, dated, with the homeowner's permission by text. Four minutes of work. It answers the exact question the buyer was not going to ask out loud.

Now run the prompt. Paste your offer, meaning the buyer brief, the result statement, and the scope card, plus every claim from your inventory with its evidence. It labels each claim and recommends the smallest ethical proof asset for each gap. Check its labels against your own. If it calls something verified and you cannot find the file, it is not verified.

## Worked example

Pine Curve Lawn and Landscape, Gilmer. Fictional. Dale pulled every claim off his website, his truck doors, and his Facebook page, listed his evidence next to each one, and pasted the offer and the list into the exact working prompt.

Example output:

```text
"Serving Gilmer since 2019." Verified. Source: state filing, first invoice. Keep.
"14 properties under contract." Verified. Source: signed agreements. Keep, update quarterly.
"Fully insured." Verified. Source: current certificate. Offer to show it on request.
"We have never missed a scheduled visit." Supported but incomplete. Visit logs begin January 2025. Rewrite: "No missed scheduled visits since we began logging in January 2025."
"Gilmer's most reliable lawn service." Unsupported. Remove.
"Boards love working with us." Unsupported. Remove, or replace with one named quote with written permission.
Smallest next asset: one sample monthly walk-through report from a current property, name blurred, board permission in writing.
```

Review against the pass standard. Can every public claim be traced to evidence, or is it clearly labeled as an example, estimate, or opinion? After the two removals, yes. The "never missed" line was the important catch. It was true in Dale's head and unprovable before 2025.

The one revision: Dale did not just delete "most reliable." He replaced that spot on the page with the logged fact and a date: "No missed scheduled visits since January 2025, with a photo log for every one." Same idea, now checkable by anyone who asks.

Then he texted the president of the board he has served longest and asked permission to share a blurred copy of last month's report. She said yes in eleven minutes. He saved the text in the inventory as the source and put "Friday" next to the asset. That is the difference between a proof plan and a wish list. Every line has a link, and the next asset has a date.

## Exact working prompt

```text
Audit the proof for this offer: [paste offer and evidence]. Label each claim verified, supported but incomplete, or unsupported. Recommend the smallest ethical proof asset for each gap.
```

## Guided practice

1. Copy every claim off your website, truck, signs, and social pages into the workbook Proof inventory.
2. Next to each one, write the evidence and paste a link or file name; leave it blank if there is nothing.
3. Paste your offer and the list into the exact working prompt in ChatGPT or Claude and compare its labels to yours.
4. Delete one unsupported claim from a live page today and put a dated fact in its place.
5. Name the next proof asset, get the permission you need in writing, and put a due date on it.

## Assignment and evidence

Create a proof inventory with source links and remove one unsupported claim.

Save the source material, the prompt, the first result, the final result, and your review notes.

## Pass standard

Every public claim can be traced to evidence or is clearly labeled as an example, estimate, or opinion.

## Common mistakes

- Treating star ratings as the only proof. Fix: counts, dates, documents, and dated photos come first.
- Softening a claim instead of removing it. Fix: if you cannot show it, delete it and put a fact in its place.
- Naming clients or showing their property without asking. Fix: permission in writing, even a text, saved as the source.
- Planning a big case study instead of a small sample. Fix: make the thing you can finish alone this week.
- Labeling from memory. Fix: verified means you clicked the link while you labeled it.

## Recording plan

**Target runtime:** 5 to 8 minutes.
**Primary visual:** Claim to evidence ladder.
**Screen recording:** Show the proof inventory spreadsheet with claim, evidence, source, and label columns, delete "Gilmer's most reliable" from the live page, and paste the permission text into the source cell.
**Course map:** the course visual below anchors where this lesson sits.

![The Offer Engine course visual](/images/academy/offer-engine.svg)

## Downloadable

[Open the Offer Engine workbook](/downloads/operator-academy/offer-engine-workbook.pdf)

Workbook section: OA03 Offer Builder Workbook, Proof inventory.

## Lesson check

Complete the practice, mark the assignment, and score at least 80 percent on the objective lesson check. Retakes are allowed. Major builds and capstones require reviewer approval before the completion credential can be issued.
$lesson$
from public.courses c where c.id = l.course_id and c.slug = 'offer-engine' and l.slug = 'build-the-proof-plan';

update public.lessons l set content = $lesson$# OE L05: Price for Delivery

## What you will finish

Set a price floor from real delivery cost, risk, support, and margin. When you are done you will have a completed price-floor worksheet with the math visible, and two numbers chosen: a founding price for your first few buyers and a regular price after that.

## Why this matters

The $150 job that took six hours. Then two callbacks. Then a text on Sunday night. Then a two-star review anyway, because the buyer thought $150 bought a lot more than it did. Nobody set out to lose money on that job. It was priced on a guess and a fear of hearing "too much."

Most owners I meet price one of two ways. They look at what the guy down the road charges, or they pick a number that feels safe. Neither one knows what it costs you to deliver. So the price is high enough to lose a few bids and low enough to lose money on the ones you win. That is the worst of both.

A price floor fixes the bottom. It is the number below which you are paying to work. You may price above it for lots of reasons. You never go below it, and knowing exactly why you never go below it is what lets you say the number without flinching.

Bring the scope card from lesson three and the proof inventory from lesson four. The scope tells you the hours. The proof tells you how much risk you are carrying. Price comes after both, and before objections, because price is the first objection you will hear.

## The lesson

### 1. Calculate labor and hard cost

Start with what the scope card actually takes to deliver. Not what you hope. What it took last time.

How to do it: go deliverable by deliverable. Write the hours for each. Multiply by an hourly value that includes what it really costs to have that person working: wages plus payroll taxes plus insurance. If it is you, use the rate you would have to pay someone competent to replace you. Then list hard costs: materials, fuel, software seats, dump fees, subcontractors, printing.

What good looks like: a short table on the workbook Pricing math page. "Schedule: 2 hours. Weekly visits: 22 crew hours per month. Photo log: inside the visit. Fuel and equipment reserve: $80 per month."

The mistake: using base wages as the hourly value. A crew member at $18 an hour costs closer to $24 to $26 with taxes and workers' comp. Price on $18 and you lose every hour. And pricing your own time at zero is the most common mistake in this whole course.

Example: Caddo Plumbing, Longview, fictional. Water heater replacement. Labor: two techs, 3.5 hours each, seven hours at a loaded rate of $38, so $266. Hard costs: the unit, fittings, permit fee, and disposal, $780 at cost. That is $1,046 before risk, support, or margin. The owner had been quoting $1,150 and wondering why the month never added up.

### 2. Add risk and support allowance

Every offer has hours nobody bills. The follow-up call. The "quick question" text. The rain week you make up. The client who takes five days to answer. That is support and risk, and it is real cost.

How to do it: support hours are the time you spend on the account outside the deliverables. Estimate them honestly from your last three jobs, then multiply by your hourly value. Risk allowance is a percentage on top of everything for what goes wrong: rework, a callback, a part that fails, a month with two storms. I use 10 percent for work I have done fifty times and 20 percent or more for work I have done twice.

What good looks like: "Support: 2 hours per month at $45, $90. Risk: 10 percent." Written down. So the next time a buyer asks why you are higher than the Facebook bid, you answer with a line item instead of a shrug.

The mistake: skipping this because "it is just a few texts." Pull your phone log for one account. Count them.

Example: Reyes Bookkeeping, Kilgore, fictional. The 90-day cleanup. Labor: 40 hours at $65, $2,600. Software seat, $120. Support: six hours of calls and chasing receipts, $390. Risk: 15 percent, because half her clients' books are worse than they admit on the first call. That is about $3,577 before margin. Her old price was $2,500 flat. She had been doing a thousand dollars of free work per client and calling it customer service.

### 3. Choose a testable price

The floor is cost plus a margin you choose. Above it, you are picking a price to test, not a price to defend forever.

How to do it: state your margin assumption as a number. Twenty-five percent on cost is a common starting point for service work. Write it down as an assumption, because that is what it is. Then pick two prices. A founding price for your first three to five buyers, still above the floor, in exchange for something you need: permission to photograph, a written reference, a fast decision. And a regular price after that. Put a real end condition on the founding price, "first three properties" or "through October." Not a fake countdown. A limit you will honor.

Then package it good, better, best if it helps the buyer choose. Good is the scope card. Better adds the one thing buyers keep asking for. Best adds one more. Do not pretend you know the market wants three tiers. You find that out in lesson eight.

What good looks like: "Floor $1,020 per month. Founding $1,050, first three properties, includes permission to photograph and a written reference. Regular $1,250."

The mistake: skipping the floor and going straight to "what will they pay." What they will pay is a question for the validation week, and you will only hear the answer clearly if you already know your bottom.

The calculators at /tools are free. Use them to check your math, not to replace it.

Now run the prompt with your real inputs in the brackets. It shows the math and gives good-better-best packaging. Check every line against your worksheet. If the model's hours look prettier than yours, yours are right.

## Worked example

Pine Curve Lawn and Landscape, Gilmer. Fictional. Dale took his scope card, pulled his crew's hours off the last two months of timesheets for a similar property, and filled the brackets: labor hours 24, hourly value $26 loaded, hard costs $80 per month, support hours 2 at $45, risk allowance 10 percent. He pasted the exact working prompt into ChatGPT.

The first pass came back with a floor of $1,092. Reading the labor line, he saw the problem. His 24 crew hours already included the monthly walk-through, and support counted it again. He moved the walk-through to support only, changed labor to 22, and ran it again.

Example output:

```text
Labor: 22 hours x $26 = $572
Hard costs: $80
Support: 2 hours x $45 = $90
Direct cost: $742
Risk allowance 10%: $74. Cost with risk: $816
Margin assumption 25% on cost: $204. Price floor: $1,020 per property per month
Good: schedule, weekly visits, photo log, contact line, walk-through. Better: adds a quarterly bed refresh. Best: adds seasonal color and one storm cleanup per quarter. Market demand is unknown; test one tier before committing to three.
```

Review against the pass standard. Does the selected price cover direct cost, support, risk, and a stated margin assumption? Direct cost $742 with support inside it, risk $74, margin stated at 25 percent. Yes.

The one revision was catching that double count, which dropped the floor by $72. Then he chose his numbers. Founding: $1,050 per month for the first three properties, in exchange for written permission to photograph the common areas and one reference. Regular: $1,250 per month.

Then he did the thing that makes it real. He said "twelve fifty a month" out loud to his wife across the kitchen table. Twice. The second time it came out flat and normal. That is when a price is ready to test.

## Exact working prompt

```text
Help me calculate a price floor for [offer]. Use these inputs: labor hours [x], hourly value [x], hard costs [x], support hours [x], risk allowance [x percent]. Show the math and give good-better-best packaging without pretending to know market demand.
```

## Guided practice

1. Go deliverable by deliverable on your scope card and write real hours from your last three jobs, not estimates from hope.
2. Set a loaded hourly value that includes taxes and insurance, and price your own time at what a replacement would cost.
3. Count support hours from one real account's call and text log, and choose a risk percentage you can defend.
4. Paste your inputs into the exact working prompt in ChatGPT or Claude and check every line of the math against the workbook Pricing math page.
5. Write your margin assumption, then choose a founding price with a real end condition and a regular price, both above the floor.

## Assignment and evidence

Complete the price-floor worksheet and select a founding and regular price.

Save the source material, the prompt, the first result, the final result, and your review notes.

## Pass standard

The selected price covers direct cost, support, risk, and a stated margin assumption.

## Common mistakes

- Pricing your own hours at zero. Fix: use the rate you would pay someone competent to replace you.
- Using base wages instead of loaded cost. Fix: add payroll tax and insurance before you multiply.
- Skipping support because it is "just a few texts." Fix: count them on one real account for one month.
- A founding price below the floor to land the first client. Fix: founding is a discount from regular, never below the floor.
- A fake deadline on the founding price. Fix: name the real limit, three clients or a date, and honor it.

## Recording plan

**Target runtime:** 5 to 8 minutes.
**Primary visual:** Price floor stack.
**Screen recording:** Fill the Pricing math worksheet on screen line by line with the Pine Curve numbers, catch the double-counted walk-through, and write the founding and regular prices at the bottom.
**Course map:** the course visual below anchors where this lesson sits.

![The Offer Engine course visual](/images/academy/offer-engine.svg)

## Downloadable

[Open the Offer Engine workbook](/downloads/operator-academy/offer-engine-workbook.pdf)

Workbook section: OA03 Offer Builder Workbook, Pricing math.

## Lesson check

Complete the practice, mark the assignment, and score at least 80 percent on the objective lesson check. Retakes are allowed. Major builds and capstones require reviewer approval before the completion credential can be issued.
$lesson$
