update public.lessons l set content = $lesson$# OE L06: Answer Real Objections

## What you will finish

Respond to hesitation with clarity, proof, fit, and boundaries. When you are done you will have an objection bank of eight answers in your own words, tested on one real person, ready to become the FAQ section of your offer page.

## Why this matters

The buyer says "let me think about it," and you never hear from them again. You tell yourself they were not serious. Most of the time they had one question they did not feel comfortable asking, and you never gave them a place to ask it.

There are two bad ways to handle that. The first is to say nothing and hope. The second is pressure. A countdown. "Price goes up Friday." A third follow-up text that reads like a collections call. Pressure works on a few people once. Then they refund, or they tell three friends, and in a town of forty thousand that is a lot of friends.

The honest way is to know the eight things people actually hesitate about and answer them before they have to ask. Not with a script. With the truth, including the limits. Bring the proof inventory from lesson four and the price-floor worksheet from lesson five. The two biggest objections are "can I trust you" and "is it worth it," and you now have real answers to both.

This comes before the offer page because the FAQ is built from this bank, not from what a marketer imagines people ask.

## The lesson

### 1. Collect exact language

Do not write objections from memory. Memory cleans them up. Collect them the way people actually said them.

How to do it: open your text messages, your email, your Quo call log or whatever you use, your Facebook messages, and your quote follow-ups. Look for the moment someone slowed down. Copy the exact words. "Y'all are a little higher than the other guy." "I need to run it by my husband." "We got burned last time." "What if it rains?" Put them on the workbook Objection bank page, one per line, word for word, with the date and where it came from.

If you do not have eight yet, ask. Call three past buyers who said yes and ask what almost made them say no. Call two who said no and ask what the deciding thing was. People will tell you when you are not selling.

What good looks like: eight to twelve lines in the buyer's voice, not yours.

The mistake: writing objections in business language. "Concern about pricing relative to competitors" is not what anyone said. "Y'all are higher" is.

Example: Whitfield Family Dental, Marshall, fictional. The front desk kept hearing "I don't have insurance" and ending the conversation there. Once they wrote it down word for word, they realized it was never about insurance. It was "what will this cost me, and will you tell me before you start."

### 2. Identify the uncertainty

Every objection is a question underneath. Your job is to find the question, because the words on top are usually not the real thing.

How to do it: next to each line, write "what are they actually unsure about?" It is almost always one of five. Will this work for someone like me? Can I trust you to show up? What happens if it goes wrong? Is the price fair for what I get? Do I have to decide right now? Match every line to one.

What good looks like: "We got burned last time" becomes "can I trust you to show up, and what happens when you do not." "Our current guy is cheaper" becomes "is the extra money buying anything I can see." "I need to run it by the board" becomes "I cannot decide alone and I do not want to be pushed."

The mistake: answering the surface words. When someone says "too expensive" and you drop the price, you just told them the first price was made up, and you still did not answer the question they had.

Example: Reyes Bookkeeping, Kilgore, fictional. "I'm not comfortable giving you bank access." Underneath: "what can you do with that access, and who else can see it." The answer was read-only access with no ability to move money, one named person, and a one-page document that says so. Price never came up again.

### 3. Answer without pressure

Now write each answer in plain language, the way you would say it standing in the buyer's driveway. Four parts. Name the real uncertainty. Answer it directly, with a fact from your proof inventory where you have one. State the limit, the thing you do not control or do not include. Give one low-pressure next step.

How to do it: keep each answer under a hundred words. If you have a fact, use the fact. If you do not, say so. Never invent a deadline. If the founding price really ends after three clients, say that, and say it the same way to everyone.

What good looks like: "We got burned last time. I hear that from boards a lot, and it is usually about no-shows. Every visit gets a signed checklist and four to six dated photos texted to you the same day. Since we started logging in January 2025 we have not missed a scheduled visit. If one is ever missed, it is made up within three business days, in writing. I do not control the weather, but I do control whether you hear from me. If it helps, I can send you last month's report from a current property, name blurred."

The mistake: ending with a push. "So let's get you signed up today!" Cut it. The next step should be something the buyer can say yes to without paying: see a sample, walk the property, talk to a current client, take the week.

Then test it. Read all eight to one real person who fits the buyer brief. Not a friend. Watch their face. Where they frown, the answer is not clear yet. Where they ask a follow-up question, you just found objection nine.

Example: Sabine Strength, Longview, fictional. "I've tried gyms before and quit." Real uncertainty: "will I be embarrassed and waste money again." Answer: six sessions with a coach, a written program you keep, and week one is a form check, not a workout you cannot finish. Limit: it only works if you show up for all six. Next step: come watch a session before you pay anything. The owner read it to a member's sister. She said, "Okay, that I could do." That is a pass.

Now run the prompt with your collected lines pasted in. It returns the real uncertainty, a plain answer, the limitation, and the next step for each. Rewrite every answer in your own words before it goes anywhere near your page.

## Worked example

Pine Curve Lawn and Landscape, Gilmer. Fictional. Dale went through six months of texts and quote follow-up emails and pulled eight lines, word for word: "Our current guy is cheaper." "We got burned by the last company." "What happens when it rains all week?" "Are you insured?" "We don't want to be locked into twelve months." "Who do we actually call when something's wrong?" "Can you also do the trees and the sprinklers?" "The board has to vote, I can't decide today."

He pasted all eight into the exact working prompt in ChatGPT.

Example output:

```text
Objection: "We don't want to be locked into twelve months."
Real uncertainty: What if you stop showing up and we are stuck paying for it.
Answer: The schedule runs twelve months so the property is planned for every season, but the agreement can be ended with 30 days written notice after the first 90 days.
Limitation: The founding price applies only to twelve-month schedules. Month-to-month is regular price.
Next step: Read the one-page agreement first. No signature needed to see it.
```

Review against the pass standard. Does the answer address the concern directly? Yes. The fear is being stuck, and the 30-day exit answers it. Does it include limits? Yes, the founding price condition. Does it manufacture urgency? No.

The one revision: the model's answer to "the board has to vote" ended with "I would be happy to attend your next meeting to close the deal." Dale cut "close the deal." His version: "Boards vote. That is how it should work. I can send a one-page summary you can forward, and if it helps I will come to the meeting and answer questions for fifteen minutes. Take the time you need."

Then he read all eight to the manager of the office park on Highway 271, the third name from his buyer brief. She frowned at the rain answer. "Three business days is a long time in May." He changed it to "made up within three business days, and during growing season you move to the front of the next dry day." Objection answered, and he had not spent a dollar on ads yet.

## Exact working prompt

```text
Turn these buyer objections into honest FAQ answers: [paste objections]. For each, identify the real uncertainty, answer in plain language, state any limitation, and give a low-pressure next step.
```

## Guided practice

1. Pull eight objections word for word from your texts, emails, call log, and quote follow-ups, and paste them onto the workbook Objection bank page.
2. Next to each one, write the real uncertainty in one line.
3. Paste the eight into the exact working prompt in ChatGPT or Claude and read what comes back.
4. Rewrite every answer in your own voice with a fact from your proof inventory, a limit, and one no-commitment next step.
5. Read all eight to one real person who fits your buyer brief and change anything that made them frown.

## Assignment and evidence

Write eight objection answers and test them with one real person.

Save the source material, the prompt, the first result, the final result, and your review notes.

## Pass standard

Answers address the concern directly, include limits, and do not manufacture urgency.

## Common mistakes

- Writing objections from memory in your own words. Fix: copy the exact text from a real message.
- Dropping the price when someone says "too much." Fix: answer what the money buys, with a line item from the worksheet.
- Ending every answer with a push. Fix: the next step is something they can say yes to without paying.
- Answering the surface words instead of the uncertainty. Fix: write "what are they actually unsure about" first.
- Testing on a friend. Fix: test on someone who fits the brief and has no reason to be nice to you.

## Recording plan

**Target runtime:** 5 to 8 minutes.
**Primary visual:** Objection response matrix.
**Screen recording:** Show the eight raw objections pasted from real texts onto the Objection bank page, the prompt output beside them, then Dale's rewritten "board has to vote" answer with "close the deal" deleted.
**Course map:** the course visual below anchors where this lesson sits.

![The Offer Engine course visual](/images/academy/offer-engine.svg)

## Downloadable

[Open the Offer Engine workbook](/downloads/operator-academy/offer-engine-workbook.pdf)

Workbook section: OA03 Offer Builder Workbook, Objection bank.

## Lesson check

Complete the practice, mark the assignment, and score at least 80 percent on the objective lesson check. Retakes are allowed. Major builds and capstones require reviewer approval before the completion credential can be issued.
$lesson$
from public.courses c where c.id = l.course_id and c.slug = 'offer-engine' and l.slug = 'answer-objections';

update public.lessons l set content = $lesson$# OE L07: Write the Offer Page

## What you will finish

Assemble the buyer, problem, result, scope, proof, price, and next action into one page. When you are done you will have a full offer page draft in a document you own, read aloud once, with one call to action, ready to publish on a page you control.

## Why this matters

I have sat with owners whose websites have fourteen pages and no offer. Services, about, gallery, blog, contact. A buyer lands, reads for forty seconds, and cannot tell what to buy, what it costs, or what to do next. So they leave and call the company whose page says one thing.

Every dollar you spend on attention later, ads, yard signs, posts, referrals, lands on this page. If the page is vague, the money is wasted before it arrives. That is why I build the page before I ever run an ad, and why this lesson comes before the validation week. You need one place to send five people.

The good news: you already wrote it. The buyer brief from lesson one. The result statement from two. The scope card from three. The proof inventory from four. The prices from five. The objection bank from six. This lesson is assembly. The only new skills are order, and the discipline to have one call to action.

## The lesson

Before you start, put all six artifacts into one document. That is your approved brief. Read it top to bottom once. If anything contradicts anything else, fix it in the brief, not on the page.

### 1. Lead with the buyer's problem

The top of the page is not about you. It is about the person in the buyer brief and the problem in their words.

How to do it: headline first. One sentence a board president would recognize as her own situation. Then two or three short paragraphs that describe the problem the way it was said in the objection bank and the buyer interviews. Then the result statement from lesson two: output promised, outcome hoped for, in that order.

What good looks like: "Common areas that get done on schedule, with photos to prove it." Under it: "Your mow crew skipped a week again. Residents complained at the meeting. And you have a day job." The buyer nods. Now they keep reading.

The mistake: a headline about you. "Gilmer's trusted lawn care partner." Nobody recognizes their own life in that. The other mistake is a clever headline. Clear beats clever every time on a page a plumber reads on his phone in a parking lot.

Example: Reyes Bookkeeping, Kilgore, fictional. Old headline: "Bookkeeping, payroll, and more for small businesses." New: "Know which jobs made money before the CPA asks." Same business. Now the plumbing owner reading it feels seen.

### 2. Show the deliverable and process

This is the middle of the page. Deliverables, process, proof, fit and non-fit, price, FAQ, limitations. In that order.

How to do it: paste the five deliverables from the scope card, each as one line with its format. Then the process as three to five numbered steps from first contact to done, with time on each. Then proof, facts only, from the inventory, with dates. Then "this is for" and "this is not for" from the buyer brief. Then the price, with the founding condition stated plainly and the regular price next to it. Then the eight answers from the objection bank as the FAQ. Then the limitations statement, in the same voice as everything else.

What good looks like: a buyer can answer every question they had without calling. What do I get. How does it go. Why should I believe you. Is this for me. What does it cost. What about my worry. What do you not do.

The mistake: hiding the price. If you make people call for the price, the ones who would have paid it assume it is more than it is, and the ones who cannot afford it call anyway and cost you an hour. Put the number on the page. If the price truly varies, put the starting price and the one thing that changes it.

Example: Big Pine Roofing, Henderson, fictional. Process: 1. Free 30-minute roof inspection with photos, same week. 2. Written estimate within two business days. 3. Install scheduled within ten business days of approval. 4. Magnet sweep and photo walk-through at the end. Four steps, each with time. A homeowner can see the whole thing before she calls.

### 3. Make the next action obvious

One call to action. One. Not "call, text, email, or fill out the form." One button, one thing, repeated at the top and the bottom.

How to do it: choose the next step that matches how your buyer decides. Boards and property managers want a walk-through. Homeowners want an inspection. A contractor with messy books wants a fifteen-minute call. Name it, say how long it takes, and say what happens after. Make the button say the thing: "Book a 20-minute property walk." Never "Submit."

Then decide where the page lives. On a site you own, not a social profile you rent. If you do not have a page you control yet, the free build at /free-build exists for exactly this: one page, on your domain, that you own.

What good looks like: the same button at the top and the bottom, a booking link or a short form behind it, and a plain sentence next to it about what happens next. "You will get a text to confirm the time, and I will bring last month's sample report."

The mistake: the second option. Every extra choice on the page is a place to stop. If you must offer a phone number for people who hate forms, fine, but it leads to the same next step, a walk-through, just booked by phone.

Example: Whitfield Family Dental, Marshall, fictional. The old page had "Call now," "Request appointment," "Ask a question," and a chat bubble. The new page has one: "Book a new patient visit." The front desk gets the same conversation every time.

Now run the prompt with your approved brief pasted in. It returns a page with every section. Then do the part most people skip. Read it aloud, start to finish, into your phone's voice recorder. Every place you stumble is a sentence to cut. Every place you sound like a brochure is a sentence to rewrite in your own words. Then revise once.

## Worked example

Pine Curve Lawn and Landscape, Gilmer. Fictional. Dale put the buyer brief, result statement, scope card, proof inventory, price worksheet, and objection bank into one Google Doc and read it through. He found one contradiction: the result statement from lesson two still said next-business-day response, and the scope card from lesson three said same-day during business hours. He kept the scope card's version and fixed the result statement. Then he pasted the whole brief into the exact working prompt.

Example output:

```text
Headline: Common areas that get done on schedule, with photos to prove it.
Problem: Your mow crew skipped a week again. Residents complained at the meeting. You have a day job, and you are chasing a vendor on your lunch break.
Outcome: Within 30 days you will have a written 12-month schedule, your first two visits completed with dated photos, and one number that answers, so you stop being the one who chases.
Deliverables: 12-month schedule (PDF). Weekly visit with signed checklist. Photo log after every visit. Monthly walk-through with a one-page report. One contact line, same-day response in business hours.
Proof: Trusted by boards across Upshur County. Fully insured. Serving Gilmer since 2019.
Price: $1,250 per month. Founding price $1,050 per month for the first three properties, with permission to photograph and one written reference.
Call to action: Book a 20-minute property walk. Or call for a quote.
```

Review against the pass standard. Does the page match the approved brief? Every line traced back to an artifact except one. Does it use one call to action? No. The walk button at the top and "or call for a quote" at the bottom is two. Does it make any unsupported claim? "Trusted by boards across Upshur County" is not in the proof inventory. It sounds good, and it is not a fact.

The one revision: Dale cut "or call for a quote," repeated the walk button at the bottom, and replaced the Upshur County line with "14 properties under contract, no missed scheduled visits since January 2025." Then he read the whole page aloud into his phone. Four minutes. He cut nine sentences that sounded like a website and kept the ones that sounded like him talking to a board president. The page got shorter and better.

## Exact working prompt

```text
Write a one-page offer page using this approved brief: [paste brief]. Include headline, problem, outcome, deliverables, process, proof, fit and non-fit, price, FAQ, limitations, and one call to action. Use short sentences and no hype.
```

## Guided practice

1. Paste the six artifacts from lessons one through six into one document and fix any line that contradicts another.
2. Paste the brief into the exact working prompt in ChatGPT or Claude and put the result into a fresh document.
3. Check every claim on the page against the proof inventory and delete anything with no source.
4. Cut to one call to action, name it as a verb, and repeat it at the top and the bottom.
5. Read the page aloud once into your phone, then cut every sentence you stumbled on.

## Assignment and evidence

Draft the full offer page and read it aloud once before revising.

Save the source material, the prompt, the first result, the final result, and your review notes. This is a designated deliverable lesson. Use the submission panel below to send the viewable link for review.

## Pass standard

The page matches the approved brief, uses one call to action, and makes no unsupported claim.

## Common mistakes

- A headline about you instead of the buyer's problem. Fix: write the first line in words from your objection bank.
- Price hidden behind "contact us." Fix: put the number on the page with the founding condition next to it.
- Three ways to get in touch. Fix: one next step, one button, repeated twice.
- New claims that appear during drafting. Fix: every sentence in the proof section needs a line in the inventory.
- Publishing on a page you rent. Fix: put it on a domain you own; use /free-build if you have nothing yet.

## Recording plan

**Target runtime:** 5 to 8 minutes.
**Primary visual:** Offer page anatomy.
**Screen recording:** Show the six artifacts stacked into one brief, the prompt run, the page pasted into a Google Doc, then a read-aloud on camera with the second call to action and the "trusted by boards" line deleted.
**Course map:** the course visual below anchors where this lesson sits.

![The Offer Engine course visual](/images/academy/offer-engine.svg)

## Downloadable

[Open the Offer Engine workbook](/downloads/operator-academy/offer-engine-workbook.pdf)

Workbook section: OA03 Offer Builder Workbook, Offer page wireframe.

## Lesson check

Complete the practice, mark the assignment, and score at least 80 percent on the objective lesson check. Retakes are allowed. Major builds and capstones require reviewer approval before the completion credential can be issued.
$lesson$
from public.courses c where c.id = l.course_id and c.slug = 'offer-engine' and l.slug = 'write-the-offer-page';

update public.lessons l set content = $lesson$# OE L08: Validate Before Scaling

## What you will finish

Run a small evidence-gathering launch before spending heavily. When you are done you will have a seven-day validation plan, a filled scorecard from five real conversations or five qualified page visits, and one written decision: continue, revise, or stop.

## Why this matters

An owner in Longview, fictional, put $2,000 into Facebook ads pointed at a page nobody had ever bought from. Twelve leads. Zero sales. He concluded that ads do not work. The ads worked fine. They sent twelve strangers to an offer that had never been said out loud to a human.

That is the cost of skipping this lesson. Not the $2,000. The wrong conclusion, which costs a year.

Everything you built in this course is a set of guesses. Good guesses, built from evidence, but guesses. The buyer brief, the result, the scope, the price, the answers, the page. Seven days and five conversations will tell you more than a month of rewriting.

This is the last lesson. Bring the offer page from lesson seven. This is where you find out.

## The lesson

Two rules before we start. First, permissioned outreach only. People who know you, who asked to hear from you, or who you can reach through a channel they chose. No bought lists, no scraped numbers, no cold texts. If you are unsure whether a contact is fair, they are not. If any of your outreach is by text, have your attorney review your consent language once and move on. Second, owned channels only. Your page, your Google Business Profile, your email list, your phone, your truck. No paid ads this week. Ads come later, in The Local Ads Operator, after you have proof.

### 1. Choose a small audience

Five is the number. Five conversations with people who fit the buyer brief, or five qualified visits to the page from people who fit it. Not fifty. Five you can reach and learn from.

How to do it: go back to the three names under your buyer brief. Add two more from your phone: past buyers, people who asked for a quote and said "keep me posted," a current client who could introduce you to one more. Write down how you will reach each one and why that channel is fair. If you would rather test the page than talk, post it once to your Google Business Profile and your own social page, and send it to your existing list with a plain note. A qualified visit is someone who fits the brief and reached the call to action. Not a cousin being nice.

What good looks like: five names, a channel next to each, a date inside the next seven days, and a permission note. "Referred by Tim at Timberline, he said to call."

The mistake: going wide to feel productive. Twenty strangers teach you less than five right people, and they cost goodwill in a small town.

Example: Reyes Bookkeeping, Kilgore, fictional. Her five: two current clients who fit, one contractor a client offered to introduce, one owner who asked for a quote in March and said to follow up, and one from her church who brought it up himself. All permissioned, all reachable this week, no list needed.

### 2. Define pass and fail signals

Decide what counts before you start, or you will grade the week on how you feel on Friday.

How to do it: write three or four signals with numbers. Problem recognition: how many of the five describe the problem in their own words before you lead them? Fit: how many say "that's me" or name someone who is? Price: how many react to the number with a question instead of a laugh? Action: how many take the next step, a walk-through, a sample, a booking? For a first test I set the line at three of five recognize the problem, two of five take the next step, and nobody rejects the price without giving a reason. Below that, revise. If nobody recognizes the problem, stop and go back to lesson one. That is not failure. That is a week well spent.

What good looks like: the thresholds written at the top of the workbook Validation scorecard before the first call, and the decision rule under them. Continue if all pass. Revise if one fails. Stop and rethink the buyer if problem recognition fails.

The mistake: measuring compliments. "Sounds great" is not a signal. A calendar invite is. A question about the contract is. "Can you do my brother's place too" is.

For an outside read on the business, the Business Growth Diagnostic at /diagnostic is free and takes a few minutes.

Example: Sabine Strength, Longview, fictional. Pass signals for the six-week intro: three of five say they have quit a gym before and why, two of five book the free watch-a-session visit, at least one asks what happens after week six. They tested with five people from their member referral list. Four booked. The one who did not said the mornings were wrong. That became a second time slot, not a new offer.

### 3. Record questions and decisions

The conversations are the product of this week. Write them down like evidence, because they are.

How to do it: the same five questions to everyone, in the same order. When did this problem last cost you something? What did you try? What would have to be true for you to fix it this month? Here is what I do and what it costs, what is your first question? What would make this an easy yes? Then record, the same day, on the scorecard: their exact words, the objection they raised, whether it was already in your bank, what they did next, and what you changed.

At the end of day seven, write the decision in one line and the reason in one more. Continue: open the founding offer to the first three. Revise: name the one thing that changes, and run five more. Stop: go back to the lesson where the signal failed.

What good looks like: five rows, exact words, a next action for each, and a dated decision at the bottom. Plus a list of the new objections, because they go straight into your bank and onto your page.

The mistake: not writing it down because you will remember. You will remember the compliment. You will forget the question that would have changed the offer.

Now run the prompt with your offer pasted in. It returns a day-by-day plan, the questions, thresholds, what to record, and the decision at the end. Change the thresholds to your own numbers. Then start on day one, not next week.

When the week is over, look at what you are holding. A buyer brief, a result statement, a scope card, a proof inventory, a price floor, an objection bank, an offer page, and a scorecard with real words on it. That is an offer. Most businesses in this county have never had one written down. The next course is The Lead Capture System, where you build the free thing that brings the right people to this page and the form that puts them into a record you own. The Follow-Up Engine comes after that, for the people who raised a hand this week. Finish the week first.

## Worked example

Pine Curve Lawn and Landscape, Gilmer. Fictional. Dale pasted his offer page into the exact working prompt in ChatGPT on a Sunday night.

Example output:

```text
Day 1: Text the three named boards and two referrals to ask for a 20-minute property walk this week. Post the page once to your Google Business Profile.
Days 2 to 5: Walk properties. Ask the five questions in order. Record answers the same day.
Day 6: Send each person a one-page summary and the sample report. Record replies.
Day 7: Score the week and write the decision.
Pass thresholds: 3 of 5 describe the missed-visit problem unprompted; 2 of 5 request a proposal or agreement; no price rejection without a stated reason.
Decision: continue to the first three founding properties, revise one element, or stop and revisit the buyer.
```

Review against the pass standard. Fixed window: seven days, Sunday to Sunday. Measurable signals: three thresholds with numbers. Consent-respecting outreach: three boards he already serves or who requested quotes, two warm introductions with the introducer's permission, and his own Google Business Profile. Clear decision: continue, revise, or stop, defined before the first walk.

What happened: five walks in six days. Four described the problem unprompted, and three of them used the word "chase." Two asked for the agreement that week. One said $1,250 was above what the board had budgeted and asked whether a twice-monthly winter schedule could bring it down. New objection, straight into the bank. The office park manager on 271 asked whether he could handle the parking lot islands, which were not on the scope card.

The one revision: he added a winter schedule option to the price sheet, quoted separately, and left the summer scope alone. Decision, written on the scorecard Sunday night: continue. Open the founding price to the first three properties. Two signed by the following Friday.

He never ran an ad. He ran five conversations, wrote them down, and decided.

## Exact working prompt

```text
Create a seven-day validation plan for [offer]. Use only permissioned outreach and owned channels. Define daily actions, conversation questions, pass/fail thresholds, what to record, and the decision at the end.
```

## Guided practice

1. Write five names who fit your buyer brief, the channel you will use for each, and why that contact is fair.
2. Paste your offer page into the exact working prompt in ChatGPT or Claude and replace its thresholds with your own numbers.
3. Copy the thresholds and the decision rule onto the workbook Validation scorecard before the first conversation.
4. Run the five conversations or send the five page invitations, asking the same questions in the same order, and record exact words the same day.
5. On day seven, write the decision in one line, the reason in one more, and the date.

## Assignment and evidence

Run five buyer conversations or five qualified page visits and record what happened.

Save the source material, the prompt, the first result, the final result, and your review notes. This is a designated deliverable lesson. Use the submission panel below to send the viewable link for review.

## Pass standard

The test has a fixed window, measurable signals, consent-respecting outreach, and a clear continue, revise, or stop decision.

## Common mistakes

- Going wide with cold outreach to feel productive. Fix: five people who know you or asked to hear from you.
- Setting the pass line after the week is over. Fix: thresholds on the scorecard before the first call.
- Counting compliments as signals. Fix: a booking, a signed agreement, or a specific question is a signal.
- Changing three things at once after the test. Fix: revise one element and run five more.
- Running ads to an untested page. Fix: five humans first, then The Local Ads Operator.

## Recording plan

**Target runtime:** 5 to 8 minutes.
**Primary visual:** Seven-day evidence loop.
**Screen recording:** Show the seven-day plan on the Validation scorecard, fill in two rows from real conversations with exact words, then write the continue decision and the new winter-schedule objection at the bottom.
**Course map:** the course visual below anchors where this lesson sits.

![The Offer Engine course visual](/images/academy/offer-engine.svg)

